"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIt was just a matter of painting a little on a whole piece of healthy skin - I'll tell you, I was shocked. If there were a wound on the skin or if it was ingested internally, that would be another matter. So, you can imagine what would happen to Field after swallowing this stuff - he drank a lot of it!\" [1335] Ellery's eyebrows furrowed in thought. He began to wipe the lenses of his spectacles. [1336] \"This is not all,\" Dr. Jones continued. \"As far as I know \u2013 I've been working in this city for years and I have some knowledge of developments in other parts of the world \u2013 but as far as I know, tetraethyl lead has never been used for criminal purposes.\" [1337] The detective sat up straight, his eyes widening. \"That's significant, doctor!\" he murmured. \"Are you sure?\" [1338] \"Of course. That's why I'm so interested.\" [1339] \"How long would it take to kill someone with this poison, doctor?\" Ellery asked slowly. [1340] Dr. Jones looked grim. \"I can't give you an exact answer because, as far as I know, no one has died from this poison before. But I can make a fairly accurate guess. I think Field would not have lasted more than fifteen minutes, maybe twenty, after ingesting this poison.\" [1341] Quin's cough broke the ensuing silence. \"On the other hand, doctor,\" he said, \"isn't the uniqueness of this poison what makes it easy to trace? What do you think is its most common source? Where does it come from? If I wanted to use it for criminal purposes without leaving a trace, how would I get it?\" [1342] The toxicologist's face twisted into a bitter smile. \"Tracking down this poison,\" he said earnestly, \"is up to you. You can look it up. What I can confirm right now - remember, it's almost completely new to us - tetraethyl lead is most commonly found in a certain type of petroleum product. I stumbled upon the simplest method to produce it in large quantities. You won't guess how. It can be extracted from the most common and ordinary gasoline.\" [1343] The Quin father and son exclaimed in a low voice, \"Gasoline!\" The detective shouted, \"Ah - but how are we supposed to trace it?\" [1344] \"The problem is here,\" the toxicologist replied. \"I can go to a gas station on the corner, fill up a tank, drive home, and extract tetraethyl lead from the gas tank with a little time and effort.\" [1345] \"Does that mean, doctor,\" Ellery interjected hopefully, \"that the killer needs some experience with chemical experiments - familiarity with chemical analysis, or something like that?\" [1346] \"It doesn't say that. Anyone with a home distillation apparatus can extract tetraethyl lead, and leave no trace. The advantage of the process is that the boiling point of tetraethyl lead is higher than any other component in gasoline. All you have to do is extract the other components at a certain temperature, and what's left is this poison.\" [1347] The officer took a pinch of snuff and his fingers trembled slightly. \"I can only say \u2013 I'm in awe of this killer,\" he muttered. \"Tell me, Doctor, does one need to have knowledge of toxicology to know this? If he doesn't have a particular interest in this area \u2013 and hasn't been trained in it \u2013 how could he know this?\" [1348] Dr. Jones snorted. \"Officer, you surprise me. Your question has been answered already.\" [1349] \"What's going on? What do you mean?\" [1350] \"I just told you how to extract it, didn't I? If you heard this from a toxicologist, and had a distillation room, wouldn't you be able to extract some too? You don't need any other knowledge except knowing the boiling point of tetraethyl lead. Give up, Quin! You have no chance of tracing the killer with this poison. It's possible he overheard two toxicologists, or even two doctors who had heard of this poison, mention it. The rest is easy. I'm not saying it's definitely so, the killer could be a chemist. But I'm just telling you all the possibilities.\" [1351] \"I suppose the poison was put in the whiskey, right, doctor?\" Quine asked, looking bewildered. [1352] \"That's certain,\" the toxicologist replied. \"There was a large amount of whiskey components in his stomach. Of course, it's easy to poison someone this way. Anyway, most whiskeys bought now have an ether flavor. Moreover, Field may have drunk it down without realizing it was tainted - if he could realize it at all.\" [1353] \"He wouldn't taste it, would he?\" Ellery asked wearily. [1354] \"I've never tasted it, young man, so I'm not sure,\" Dr. Jones replied somewhat sharply. \"But I doubt he could taste it - at least not enough to raise his suspicions. And once he drank it, whether he realized it or not made no difference.\" [1355] Quine turned to Plaut, who was now asleep and his cigar had gone out. \"Hey, doctor!\" [1356] Plaut opened his eyes sleepily. \"Where are my slippers - I can't seem to find them, damn it!\" [1357] Despite the tense atmosphere, the assistant coroner's words made everyone laugh. When he realized what he had said, he smiled and said, \"That's just proof that I should go home and sleep, Quine. What else do you want to know?\" [1358] \"Tell me,\" Quine said, still trembling slightly, \"what did you find out from the whiskey analysis?\" [1359] \"Oh!\" Plaut immediately woke up. \"The whiskey in the bottle is just like the other whiskeys I've tested - nothing wrong with it. These years, my main job has been to test the components of the alcohol. The smell of whiskey on Field's body made me think he had drunk some cheap stuff. But the Scottish whiskey and rye whiskey you brought me from his apartment are both very high quality. They may have been purchased from the same place, in fact. They could be imported. Since the war, I haven't seen such high-quality alcohol in the country - except for those stored before the war... I think Wiley has already given you my report, and the ginger ale is also fine.\" [1360] Quine nodded. \"Well, that seems to be settled,\" he said gravely. \"We seem to have made no progress on tetraethyl lead. But just to be on the safe side, doctor - work with the professors here to see if there are any flaws in the distribution of the poison in the body. You two are very familiar with it, and it will be more effective than other people. It's just a shot in the dark, and it may not yield any results.\" [1361] \"That's fine,\" Ellery whispered. \"Every novelist has to write until the end.\" [1362] * * *\n[1363] \"I think,\" after the two left, Ellery said urgently, \"I should go to the bookstore and buy Faulkner's novel.\" [1364] \"Sit here!\" the policeman shouted, pulling him back to the chair. \"No. That damn book won't run away. I want you to sit here, I have a headache, and you're not going anywhere.\" [1365] Ellery sat on the cushion, sighing. \"Whenever I think about how all the investigations into human weaknesses are useless and a waste of time, my respected father puts such a responsibility on me again. Hey! What do you think?\" [1366] \"I'm not putting any responsibility on you,\" Quine growled, \"Stop exaggerating, I'm already dizzy. I want you to help me straighten out this mess of a case and see - uh, if we can find anything.\" [1367] \"I'm skeptical,\" Ellery said, \"Where do I start?\" [1368] \"You don't need to say anything,\" dad grumbled, \"Tonight, I'll talk, you listen. You might want to take some notes. [1369] \"Let's start with Field. First, I think we can safely assume that our friend didn't go to the theater Monday night for entertainment, but for business. Agreed?\" [1370] \"In my opinion, that's a given,\" Ellery said, \"How did Willy report Field's activities on Monday?\" [1371] \"Field went to the office at 9:30\u2014he usually gets there in the morning. He worked until noon. There were no private visitors all morning. At 12:00, he had lunch alone at the Webster Club, returned to the office at 1:30, and worked until 4:00\u2014he seemed to go straight home after work, as the doorman and elevator operator can confirm he was back in his apartment at 4:30. Michaels went to him at 5:00 and left at 6:00, and Willy found no other information. Field left the apartment at 7:30, dressed as we found him. I have a list of clients he saw that day, but it's not very useful.\" [1372] \"What's the reason for his low bank account balance?\" Ellery asked. [1373] \"As I suspected,\" Quinn answered, \"Field has been losing a lot of money in the stock market\u2014not to mention he also wastes money at the racetrack. For a sensible man, he's a gullible fool. Anyway, that explains why his personal account cash is so low. Moreover, perhaps it can more firmly indicate that the '50,000' we found on the promotional material refers to money. And this money, I'm sure, is somehow related to the person he was supposed to meet at the theater.\" [1374] \"Now, I think we can safely conclude that Field is very familiar with the killer. On the one hand, he accepted the drink offered to him without the slightest suspicion, not even asking; on the other hand, they arranged to meet in a place that seemed to be for concealment\u2014otherwise, why choose the theater?\" [1375] \"Well, I'll ask you the same question,\" Ellery interrupted him, pursing his lips, \"Why choose the theater as the meeting place? It's obvious that they were going to engage in a secret, criminal transaction. Why not the park, which is more concealed? Or the hotel lobby, which is also good? How do you answer that?\" [1376] \"Unfortunately, my son,\" the detective said mildly, \"Field didn't suspect anyone was going to kill him. In his mind, he just had to do his part in the transaction. In fact, it's likely that Field chose the theater as the meeting place himself. Perhaps he wanted to create an alibi for himself. We can't know for sure what he was trying to do. As for the hotel lobby - he would have risked being seen by others. And perhaps he didn't want to go to a secluded place like the park. Finally, he may have had a specific reason not to be seen with the other person. Don't forget, the ticket stubs we found show that the other person didn't go into the theater with Field. But these are all pointless speculations -\"\n[1377] Ellery smiled thoughtfully but said nothing. He thought to himself that the old man hadn't fully answered his question, and for a detective like Quin, who was more direct in his thinking, this was unusual...\n[1378] But Quin continued speaking. \"Well, we must always remember that the person who Field was dealing with may not be the killer. Of course, this is just one possibility. This crime seems to have been well-planned. But if that person isn't the killer, we need to find two people in the audience on Monday night who have a direct connection to Field's death.\" [1379] \"Morgan?\" Ellery asked casually. [1380] The detective shrugged. \"Perhaps. Why didn't he tell us that when we spoke to him yesterday afternoon? He confessed to everything else. Well, maybe he felt that paying a blackmail fee to a murdered man and being at the theater was enough indirect evidence.\" [1381] \"Let's look at this,\" Ellery said. \"We found a number '5,000' written on the flyer by the deceased, which obviously refers to money. Through Sampson and Cronin, we know that Field was a despicable man and possibly a criminal. Moreover, we learned from Morgan that he was good at extortion. Therefore, I think we can safely assume that he went to the Roman Theater on Monday night to collect or arrange the payment of the $50,000 ransom from someone we don't know yet. So far, it all makes sense, doesn't it?\" [1382] \"Continue,\" the inspector grumbled without expressing his opinion. [1383] \"Okay,\" Ellery continued, \"if we assume that the person who was being extorted and the murderer are the same person, we don't need to look for a motive anymore. The motive is clear - to stop Field from continuing to extort. However, if we assume that the murderer and the person being extorted are two different people, we have to continue looking for his motive. My view is that there is no need to do so - the murderer and the person being extorted are the same. What do you think?\" [1384] \"I tend to agree with you, Ellery,\" the inspector said. \"I only mentioned another possibility - without reaching a conclusion. So, let's continue on the assumption that the murderer and the person being extorted are the same...\"\n[1385] \"Now - I want to find out about the missing tickets.\" [1386] \"Ah \u2013 missing tickets,\" Ellery murmured. \"I wonder what you can make of that?\" [1387] \"Be serious now, you little rascal!\" Quin shouted. \"That's all I know. Overall, we have to deal with eight seats \u2013 one of which belongs to Field, and we found the stub on him; one belongs to the killer, and Flint found the stub for us; and finally, there are six empty seats, with tickets sold according to the ticket office records, but nowhere in the theater or the ticket office were the stubs found, either torn or intact \u2013 nothing at all. First, it's highly unlikely that all six tickets were in the theater on Monday evening, and then someone took them out. Remember, the search of everyone that night wasn't very thorough, and it's possible that we overlooked something small like a ticket. However, that's basically impossible. The best explanation is that either Field or the killer bought eight tickets, intending to use two and leave the other six, ensuring that they could not be disturbed during their brief transaction. In this case, the wisest course of action would be to destroy the tickets as soon as they were purchased. It could have been done by either Field or the killer, depending on who arranged the meeting. So, we must forget about those six tickets \u2013 they no longer exist, and we'll never find them. [1388] \"Next,\" the detective continued, \"we learned that Field and the person he was extorting entered the theater separately. This can be inferred from the fact that when I put the two stubs together, the tear didn't line up. If two people entered the theater together, the tickets would be handed to the usher together, and they would be torn together. This doesn't mean they didn't enter the theater at the same time; they may have come in one after the other for safety, pretending not to know each other. However, Maggie O'Connell claimed that no one sat in LL30 during the first act, and Jesse Lynch, the boy selling oranges, confirmed that no one sat there for at least ten minutes after the second act began. That means either the killer hadn't entered the theater yet or he had come in but sat somewhere else \u2013 he bought another ticket. \" [1389] Ellery shook his head. [1390] \"I realized that too, son,\" the old man said, a little irritated. \"I was just working through the thought process. What I meant to say was that it seems highly unlikely that the killer entered the theater at the time of the performance; he probably came in after the second act started and at least ten minutes later.\" [1391] \"I can prove that,\" Ellery said lazily. [1392] The officer took a pinch of snuff. \"I know - the mysterious numbers on the flyers. What do you make of them?\" [1393] 930\n[1394] 815\n[1395] 50,000\n[1396] \"We know what 50,000 represents. The other two numbers, however, do not refer to money but to time. First, '815' \u2013 the play started at 8:25. It is likely that Field arrived around 8:15 or even earlier, as he glanced at his watch for some reason. Now, let's assume that he had an appointment with someone, but that person was late. It is very likely that Field jotted down the numbers on the flyer casually \u2013 first, '50,000', indicating the transaction he was considering, including the $50,000 ransom; then 8:15, the time he was considering the matter; and finally 9:30 \u2013 the time the victim should have arrived! Writing these things down was natural for Field, as anyone with a habit of scribbling in their spare time would do so. We are lucky, as this indicates two things: first, the exact time Field met with the killer was 9:30; second, it corresponds to the time we hypothesize the murder took place. At 9:25, Lynch saw Field alive and alone; by 9:30, according to what Field wrote, the killer should have arrived, and we naturally assume the killer did arrive at the theater; and according to Dr. Jones, the poison that killed Field took 15 to 20 minutes to take effect \u2013 considering that Paskack found the body at 9:55, we can infer that the poison was taken at 9:35. If the tetrachloroethylene took 20 minutes to take effect \u2013 that would be 9:55. Of course, the killer had already left the crime scene by then. Don't forget, he couldn't have known that Mr. Paskack would suddenly stand up and leave his seat. The killer may have thought that Field's body would not be discovered until intermission, at 10:05, so Field would have been dead and unable to leave any further information. The killer was undoubtedly lucky, as he discovered Field too late and did not leave any other information, only stating that he was murdered. If Paskack had left five minutes earlier, we would have caught the cunning killer by now.\" [1397] \"Great!\" Ellery murmured, his smile full of affection, \"That was well stated, congratulations!\" [1398] \"Oh, go to hell,\" his father barked, \"I was just repeating what you said Monday night in Penczel's office \u2013 in fact, although the killer left the crime scene between 9:30 and 9:55, he was still in the theater for the rest of the evening, until we got everyone to leave.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe was sent to Elmira on a 'minor theft' charge thanks to Field's clever manipulation of the law. However, Field possessed evidence of the true crime Mickles had committed and hid it in his favorite collection, intending to use it at a later date. This Field, what a cunning man... After Mickles was released, Field used these documents to coerce Mickles into doing various tasks for him without any remorse. [2192] \"For a long time, Michael has been searching for those files. His eagerness, you can imagine. He would go to the apartment whenever he had the chance. After one failure after another, he became desperate. I have no doubt that when Field found out that Michael was rummaging through his apartment day after day, he must have been laughing behind his back... On Monday night, Michael went home to sleep as he said. But on Tuesday morning, when he read the newspaper and learned that Field had been murdered, he realized it was all over. He had to search desperately for those files - if he couldn't find them, the police might find them, and then he would be finished. So, on Tuesday morning, he risked being caught by the police and returned to Field's house. Of course, the whole story about the check was just nonsense. [2193] \"But let's go back to Barry. We found a file labeled 'Miscellaneous' in the hat, detailing an unsavory matter. To abbreviate, Stephen Barry has black blood in his veins. Born into a poor family in the South, there is concrete evidence - letters, birth certificates, and so on - proving his black heritage. As we know, Field has made a business out of pursuing such things. He somehow acquired these documents, how long ago we do not know, but it must have been a long time. He had then discovered Barry's identity, finding him a struggling actor living in poverty and penniless. Field decided to leave him be for the time being. Once Barry made it big or became famous, there would be plenty of time to extort him. What Field did not anticipate was Barry's engagement to the daughter of a millionaire, the aristocratic socialite Frances Evans-Pope. It goes without saying that if Frances Evans-Pope were to find out about Barry's mixed heritage, the consequences would be dire. Furthermore, it is crucial to note that Barry had become impoverished due to gambling. His earnings had gone into the pockets of the racecourse bookmaker's agent, and he owed a significant amount of money, which he would never be able to repay with his current means, unless he married Frances. In fact, he was so destitute that he secretly urged her to marry him as soon as possible. I have always been curious about how sincerely he treated Frances. However, to be fair, I do not believe he married her solely for the money. I think he genuinely loved her - but who doesn't? But who doesn't?\" [2194] The old man smiled as he reminisced, continuing, \"Not long ago, Field brought those documents to Barry \u2013 of course, secretly. Barry gave him all the money he could, but it was barely enough to satisfy the insatiable extortionist. With no other option, he could only keep delaying Field. But Field himself was in a bind due to gambling, trying to 'recoup' funds through one small deal after another. Barry was at a dead end, realizing that unless he made Field forever silent, he would lose everything. So he planned this murder. He understood that even if he somehow managed to gather the $50,000 Field demanded \u2013 clearly an impossible task \u2013 even if he got the original documents, Field would still ruin his hopes by spreading the word. The only way out was \u2013 eliminate Field. And he did.\" [2195] \"Black blood, huh?\" Crooning grumbled, \"Poor fellow.\" [2196] \"You couldn't tell from his looks,\" said Sampson, \"he looked just as white as you and me.\" [2197] \"Barry is not like a pure black man,\" the officer countered, \"He has one drop of black blood in his veins \u2013 just one drop, but that's enough for the Evans-Pope family... Continue on. After reading these documents, we understood everything: who did it, how it was done, and the motive. But we have to make a judgment on the evidence to convict. You can't accuse someone of murder without evidence... er, what do we have? Nothing! [2198] \"Let's discuss some useful clues that could serve as evidence. The handbag of the woman - eliminate. It's worthless, you know. The source of the poison - can't find it. By the way, Barry did it according to Dr. Jones' method - the toxicologist Jones. Barry bought regular gasoline, distilled it to get tetraethyl lead. No trace left. Another possible clue - Monte Field's hat, but it's gone... Six empty tickets - we've never seen them, and the chances of seeing them are slim. The only physical evidence - those documents - only explain the motive, but can't prove anything. With this evidence, any member of Morgan or Field's gang could have committed the crime.\" [2199] \"The only hope of securing incriminating evidence lies in sending someone to sneak into Barry's apartment at night, hoping to find the hat, tickets, or other clues, such as poison or poisonous instruments. I have engaged a specialist in burglary for this purpose, who will break into Barry's apartment on Friday night while he is at the theater performing, but no such clues have been found. The hat, tickets, and poison have all been destroyed. It is clear that Barry did it, we can be sure.\" [2200] \"I gathered a group of Monday night viewers in desperation and held a meeting in the hope that someone would remember seeing Barry that night. You know, sometimes people forget things due to tension or excitement, but then they remember later. But this time it didn't work either. The only valuable testimony was from the boy selling orange juice, who saw Field pick up a handbag used at the evening party in the alley. However, this had nothing to do with Barry. Remember, on Thursday night, when we questioned the cast members, we didn't get any direct evidence either. [2201] \"So we have a perfect, hypothetical statement of fact to present to the jury, with no concrete evidence. A clever defense lawyer could easily refute our case. It's mostly indirect evidence based on reasoning. You and I both know how much of a chance we have in court... Then, my real trouble came, and Ellery was leaving town. [2202] \"I racked my brain - I was left with little. How can I convict someone without evidence? It's so frustrating. Ellery sent me a telegram with a suggestion that helped me one last time.\" [2203] \"A suggestion?\" asked Cronin. [2204] \"He suggested that I personally engage in extortion...\"\n[2205] \"You personally extort?\" Sampson looked at him. \"I don't understand.\" [2206] \"Trust Ellery, he's good at solving puzzles.\" The police officer refutes, \"I immediately understand that the only thing I can do is fabricate evidence!\" [2207] Sampson and Cronin frown in confusion. [2208] \"It's simple,\" Quine says, \"Field was killed by an unusual poison. The reason Field was killed was because he blackmailed Barry. If Barry was blackmailed in the same situation, he would use poison again - probably the same poison. My hypothesis is reasonable, right? I don't need to say that you both know, 'once bitten, twice shy.' As for Barry, if I can lure him into using tetraethyl lead on someone else, I can catch him! This poison is almost unknown - but I don't need to explain further. You can see that if I catch him with tetraethyl lead on him, that's all the evidence I need. [2209] \"How to accomplish this feat is another matter... The situation was perfect for blackmailing. In fact, I have the original documents about Barry's background and impure blood. Barry thought these documents had been destroyed - he had no reason to suspect that the ones he got from Field were carefully forged. If I blackmail him, then his situation will be the same again. So he must resort to the same tactics. [2210] \"So I took advantage of our friend Charles Michaels. I used Michaels only because, in Barry's eyes, Michaels was Field's friend, henchman, and loyal partner, and it was reasonable for him to possess the original documents. I had Michaels write a letter, dictated by me. I had Michaels write it because Barry had dealt with Field and might recognize Michaels' handwriting. This seemed like a small problem, but I couldn't take any risks. If this plan went wrong, Barry would immediately recognize it, and I would never catch him.\" [2211] \"In the letter, I attached an original document to show that this new blackmail threat was potent. I claimed that Field had given Barry a copy - the attached document proved my point. Barry didn't doubt that Michaels was imitating his master and blackmailing him. The wording was like a final ultimatum. I set a time and place, and in short, the scheme worked...\n[2212] \"I think that's all, gentlemen. Barry came, with his faithful little syringe subcutaneously injected with tetraethyl lead, and a bottle of wine - you see, except for the location, it was exactly the same as the Field case. I instructed my man - Ritter - not to take a risk. As soon as he recognized Barry, he aimed his gun at him and sounded the alarm. Fortunately, we were almost right behind them in the bushes. Barry was desperate, and if he had had even the slightest chance, he would have killed Ritter and committed suicide.\" [2213] The officer finished speaking and sighed, leaning forward to snort some snuff, and the room fell silent. [2214] Sampson shifted in his chair. \"It sounds like a thriller, Quin,\" he said admiringly, \"but there are a few things I don't understand. For example, since tetraethyl lead is unknown to people, how did Barry discover it - to the point where he could refine it himself?\" [2215] \"Oh,\" the officer smiled, \"Ever since Jones described this poison, this has bothered me. Even after catching Barry, I was still in the dark. But this shows how stupid I am - the answer has been right in front of my nose. Do you remember, when we were at the Eves-Pope estate, a doctor named Cornish was introduced? This doctor is a private friend of the old financier, and both of them are interested in medicine. In fact, I remember Ellery asking, 'Did Eves-Pope recently donate $100,000 to the Chemical Research Foundation?' Indeed, it was. One evening a few months ago, a meeting was held at the Eves-Pope house, and Barry happened to hear about tetraethyl lead. Through Cornish's introduction, a group of scientists visited this financial giant to request funding for the foundation. The evening's conversation naturally turned to rumors in medicine and recent scientific discoveries. Barry admitted that he had overheard the council member, a famous toxicologist, introducing this poison to the members. At the time, Barry didn't think he would ever use this knowledge; when he decided to kill Field, he saw the superiority of this poison and its difficult traceability.\" [2216] \"What was the significance of the message you sent me through Louis Ponzel on Thursday night, Inspector?\" Cromwell asked curiously, \"Do you remember? You asked me to observe whether Ponzel and Ponzel knew each other when they met at Louin? As I reported to you, I asked Louin, and he denied knowing Ponzel. What's going on?\" [2217] \"Ponzel,\" the policeman whispered, \"Ponzel always piques my curiosity, Tim. When I sent him to you, remember, I hadn't made any deductions about the hat... I sent him to you out of curiosity. I thought that if Louin recognized him, it might suggest a connection between Ponzel and Field. My idea wasn't confirmed, and I didn't have high hopes to begin with. Ponzel might have known Field outside, while Louin didn't know. On the other hand, I didn't want Ponzel to hang around the theater that morning; it was beneficial for both of us for him to run errands.\" [2218] \"Oh, I hope you're satisfied with the package of newspapers I sent you according to your request,\" Cromwell smiled. [2219] \"What about the anonymous letter that Morgan received? Was it a cover-up or something else?\" Sampson asked. [2220] \"It was a deceptive trick,\" Quayne said solemnly. \"Barry explained it to me last night. He heard that Morgan threatened to kill Field. Of course, he didn't know that Field was blackmailing Morgan. But he thought that if he could trick Morgan into coming to the theater on Monday night, it would create a strong false lead. If Morgan didn't come, there would be no loss. That's how he operated: he chose ordinary cheap paper, put on gloves, and printed the letter at a print shop in a messy way, signed it with a meaningless initial, and then mailed it from the post office. He was careful with his fingerprints, so he couldn't be traced from the letter. Coincidentally, Morgan fell for it and came\n[2221] Sampson nodded. \"I have one more question. How did Barry arrange for the tickets? Was it all his doing?\" [2222] \"Of course, it was. Barry convinced Field that, for fairness, the meeting and the document exchange should take place secretly in the theater. Field agreed and was easily convinced to buy eight tickets at the ticket office. He realized that, for secrecy, he needed to buy six additional tickets. He gave Barry seven and kept one for himself, which he destroyed immediately.\" [2223] The officer stood up, smiling tiredly. \"Juna! Add some coffee,\" he whispered. [2224] Sampson waved Juna away. \"Thank you, Quin, I have to go. Cromwell and I have a lot of things to deal with regarding this gang. But I can't leave until I hear the whole story from you... Quin, old friend, I truly mean it, you did an incredible job.\" [2225] \"I've never heard of such a thing,\" Cromwell said sincerely. \"A puzzle to solve, and such a perfect reasoning from start to finish!\" [2226] \"Really?\" the officer asked calmly. \"I'm glad, gentlemen. All the credit goes to Ellery. I'm so proud of my son...\"\n[2227] Sampson and Cronin left, and Juna went back to the kitchen to wash the breakfast dishes. The officer turned to the desk, picked up a pen, and quickly read the letter he had written to his son. He sighed and continued writing. [2228] Forget what I just wrote. It's been over an hour since then. Sampson and Cronin came by, and I told them all about the work we did on the case. I've never seen such a pair! They were like children, listening to a fairy tale... As I said, I know how little I've done, and how much you've done. I'm looking forward to you finding a good girl to marry, and then the Quin family will settle down and live peacefully in Italy...\n[2229] When are you coming home? Don't think I'm rushing you, but I'm so lonely, son. I... no, I mean, I'm selfish and tired. An old relic like me needs some care. You'll be home soon, won't you? Juna sends his greetings. That mischievous fellow is washing dishes in the kitchen, making my ears ring. [2230] Love, Dad\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThrough your investigation of the night watchman and O'Connell, along with the night watchman's testimony, Jesse Lynch was in the alley, the usher confirmed this fact, and everything else \u2026 ponder on it, the killer was in the theater, without a doubt.\" [1399] \"This puts us in a temporary bind. Right now, all we can do is think about the character of the people we encountered during our investigation,\" the policeman sighed and continued, \"First \u2013 Maggie O'Connell said she didn't see anyone walking in the aisle during the second act, did she tell the truth? And she said she didn't see anyone sitting in seat LL30 all night, but we know someone should have been there from 9:30 to the ten minutes or fifteen minutes before the body was found, so was she lying?\" [1400] \"That's a tricky question, Dad.\" Ellery said solemnly, \"Because if she was lying, we lose a lot of clues. If she was lying then \u2013 God help us \u2013 she could describe or even identify, or possibly even name the killer! However, her nervousness and strange demeanor may be due to the fact that she knows that Johnny was in the theater at the time and that many police officers are eager to catch him.\" [1401] \"It sounds reasonable,\" Quinn mumbled. \"Well, what about Parsons Johnny? How did he get involved in this\u2014or was he not involved at all? We must remember, according to Morgan, that Cazzanelli and Field had a close relationship. Field was his lawyer, and perhaps he bribed Parsons to help with the irregular transactions Croaning has been investigating. If Parsons being at the theater wasn't a coincidence, was it through Field or O'Connell? Is it as they both claimed? I think, my son,\" he tugged at his beard, adding, \"I want to give Parsons Johnny a taste of the whip\u2014it won't hurt his thick skin! And that little proud O'Connell girl\u2014scaring her won't do much harm either...\"\n[1402] He took a deep breath of snuff, followed by a series of sneezes, and Ellery's sympathetic laughter accompanied him. [1403] \"And dear old Benjamin Morgan,\" the officer continued, \"the anonymous letter conveniently provided him with a ticket, the source of which was very mysterious. Is he lying about this?\" [1404] \"And then there's the most interesting lady, Mrs. Angela Roxboro... Ah! These ladies, bless them! They always muddle up a man's logic. What did she say\u2014that she'd be at Field's flat by nine-thirty? That her absence was evidence? Of course, the porter confirmed her story. But it's easy to fix a porter... For Field's business\u2014especially his private business\u2014does she know more than she's letting on? Was she lying when she said Field told her he'd be back at the flat at ten? Remember, we know the meeting at the Roman Theater was at nine-thirty\u2014did he really think he could be back home by ten? By taxi, it would take fifteen or twenty minutes on the road, leaving only ten minutes for the transaction\u2014perhaps, of course. The subway wouldn't be much faster. We must remember, this woman didn't show up at the theater all evening.\" [1405] \"Just this beautiful woman alone will keep you busy,\" Ellery said. \"It's obvious that she's hiding something. Have you noticed her brazen contempt? It's not just bluffing. She knows something, Dad. I'll be watching her closely - she'll slip up eventually.\" [1406] \"Haggstrom will take care of her.\" Quinn said absent-mindedly, \"Now, what about Michaels? He doesn't have an alibi for Monday night. But that doesn't matter. He wasn't at the theater... Did he really go to Field's apartment on Tuesday morning to look for something? We've thoroughly searched Field's apartment\u2014did we miss something? He lied about the check thing, and obviously didn't know Field was dead. He must have realized he was taking a risk coming to Field's apartment. He read the paper, he wouldn't want the police there. So, what's his reason for taking the risk?\" [1407] \"Maybe something related to his prison term\u2014I mentioned that, and he looked surprised, right?\" Ellery chuckled. [1408] \"Maybe.\" The detective replied, \"By the way, I found out from Weil about Michaels' stint in Elmira. Thomas reported it was a hush-hush case\u2014much heavier than the light sentence he got in the reformatory. Michaels had been suspected of forgery\u2014it looked serious for him. Then his lawyer, Field, cleverly defended him, and he was charged with a completely different offense\u2014something like a minor theft\u2014and that was the end of the forgery charges. This Michaels seems to have some pull\u2014we have to keep a close eye on him.\" [1409] \"I have a little theory about Michaels,\" Ellery said thoughtfully, \"but let's put that aside for now.\" [1410] Quinn seemed not to have heard Ellery. He stared into the crackling fire in the hearth. \"And Lune,\" he said, \"a man like Lune, so closely connected to his employer, claiming he knows nothing else besides what his employer mentions, seems too hard to believe. Is he hiding something? If so, he'd better pray\u2014because Croaning will soon make him reveal the truth.\" [1411] \"I like Croning,\" Ellery said, \"How does a man muster the determination to do something like that... Have you thought of a question? I want to know if Morgan knew Angela Roxo. Despite both denying it, wouldn't it be interesting if they did?\" [1412] \"Son,\" Quinn complained, \"stop worrying yourself. We have enough on our plate already, so... Ah!\" [1413] The room was quiet and comfortable. Quinn lay down, the flickering firelight illuminating him. Ellery munched on a pastry. Juno sat in a corner, eyes shining with excitement, her small body hunched as she listened to their conversation. [1414] Suddenly, in a moment of mental shift, the old man's gaze met Ellery's. [1415] \"Hats...,\" Quinn murmured, \"We always come back to the hat.\" [1416] Ellery's gaze was puzzled. \"Back to the hat is fine, Dad. Hat - hat - hat! What does the hat have to do with the case? How much do we know about the hat?\" [1417] The officer shifted in his chair. He propped his feet up, took a pinch of snuff, and continued the discussion with renewed vigor. \"Well. We can't be lazy about that damned silk hat. So far, what do we know? First, the hat is still in the theater. Isn't that interesting? After such a thorough search, we couldn't find any trace, which seems impossible... Everyone left, and there was nothing in the cloakroom; there was nothing found in the debris, nothing to suggest that the hat was torn or burned; in fact, there was no clue, no evidence to help us continue the investigation. Therefore, Ellery, in this regard, the only reasonable judgment is that we are looking in the wrong place! Further, wherever it is, it must still be in the theater, because we have been closed since Monday evening. Ellery, we will go back to the theater tomorrow and turn it upside down. Unless we see some light, I won't sleep.\" [1418] Ellery was silent for a moment. \"I'm not satisfied with what you said, Dad,\" he said finally, \"Hat - hat - something is wrong!\" [1419] The old man nodded vigorously. \"Since yesterday morning, when I had time to think about the hat, I felt that we were stuck somewhere. It's already Wednesday evening, and there's no progress. We've done all the necessary work, but we've got nothing... I stared into the fire in the fireplace, \"Everything is a mess. I know all the ins and outs, but for some reason, I can't seem to put them together - explain everything... Son, the missing piece is the key to the whole case.\" [1420] The phone rang. The officer jumped up to answer the phone. He listened carefully to a man's slow speech, commented lightly, and then hung up the phone. [1421] \"It's deep in the middle of the night, who is it? And you talk so much. Oh, you know a lot of secrets, right?\" Ellery grinned. [1422] \"It's Edmund Krueger.\" Quin said, \"You remember, I told him to go check the Roman Theater yesterday morning. He has been there for two days, yesterday and today. And he clearly reported that there was no secret hiding place in the theater. Edmund Krueger has the final say on such architectural issues, and if he says there's no hiding place, you can be sure there won't be any.\" [1423] He stood up and saw Junna squatting in the corner. \"Junna! Make the bed.\" He yelled. Junna quietly walked through the room and left with a silent smile. Quin turned to Ellery, who had taken off his coat and was fumbling with his tie. [1424] \"The first thing we have to do tomorrow morning is go to the Roman Theater and search again.\" The old man spoke firmly, \"Tell you, son \u2013 I hate wasting time! Some people had better watch out!\" [1425] Ellery wrapped his father's shoulders with his muscular arm affectionately. \"Go to sleep, you old cheat!\" He laughed. [1426] This book is compiled by \"ePUBw.COM\". \"ePUBw.COM\" provides the latest and most complete e-book downloads! [1427] Part Three\n[1428] \"Great detectives are born, not made. Like all geniuses, they do not come from the meticulously cultivated police, but from the whole of humanity. The most amazing detective I have ever known is an old, dirty shaman who has never left the jungle... This truly outstanding detective has a unique gift \u2014 extraordinary observation of events, understanding of human thinking, and insight into the human mind. And this gift is the factor that solves the problem, or the unshakable law.\" [1429] \u2014 Excerpt from \"The Hunter's Guide\" by James Redix, Jr.\n[1430] Chapter 14: The Hat Problem Grows Larger\n[1431] On Thursday, September 27, the day after the murder at the Roman Theater, Inspector Queequeg and Ellery got up early and hastily dressed. Juno was dragged out of bed and dressed in plain clothes, looking like a maid in the Queequeg household. They gobbled down a hurried breakfast in Juno's disapproving gaze. [1432] They chewed their tasteless pancakes loudly, and the old man asked Juno to put through a call to Louis Pensel. After a while, the inspector spoke kindly into the phone: \"Good morning, Pensel. I apologize for waking you up so early... Something important has happened, and we need your help. Can you come to the Roman Theater to open it for us? I told you that the theater would not be closed for long, and now it seems that you can still take advantage of the public's attention to this incident. I can't determine when we'll be able to reopen, but there's a glimmer of hope that it could be tonight. Can you lend me a hand?\" [1433] Pensel mumbled a comforting phrase sleepily. [1434] \"Can you come to the Roman Theater right away to open it for us?\" the old man continued, \"I told you that the theater wouldn't be closed for long, and now it seems that you can still take advantage of the public's attention to this incident. You know, I can't determine when we'll be able to reopen, but there's a glimmer of hope that it could be tonight. Can you lend me a hand?\" [1435] \"Great!\" Pensel's voice trembled with excitement over the phone line. \"You want me to come to the theater right away? I'll be there in half an hour \u2013 I'm not even dressed yet.\" [1436] \"Alright,\" Quin replied. \"Of course, Pencier \u2013 don't let anyone in yet. Wait for us on the sidewalk before you unlock the door, and don't tell anyone. We'll discuss it further inside the theater... Wait a moment.\" [1437] He held the phone to his chest and looked at Ellery with a questioning gaze. Ellery gestured vigorously, and Quin mouthed a name, nodding in agreement before returning to the phone. [1438] \"Now there's one more thing I need your help with, Pencier,\" he continued. \"Can you find Mrs. Philip, the enthusiastic lady? I'd like her to meet us at the theater as soon as possible.\" [1439] \"Certainly, Inspector,\" Pencier said. The inspector hung up the phone. [1440] \"Well, that's settled,\" he said, rubbing his hands together and reaching into his pocket for a snuff box. \"Ah \u2013 ah \u2013 sneeze! Thank you, Sir Walter and all the hardworking pioneers who defended the tobacco industry!\" He sneezed happily. \"We'll be there in a moment, Ellery.\" [1441] He picked up the phone again and issued a few cheerful orders to the detective headquarters before slamming the phone back onto the table and urging Ellery to put on his coat. Juno looked mournfully at them as they left. He often begged the officer to let him accompany them on their occasional trips to the secluded paths of New York, but the officer had his own ideas about raising teenagers and always refused. Juno regarded his benefactor as if he were a totemic object from the Stone Age, so he had to accept this inevitable outcome and hope for better luck in the future. [1442] It was a cold, damp day. Ellery and his father pulled up their coat collars and walked towards Broadway and the subway. Both were unusually quiet, but their faces expressed a sense of eager anticipation - an uncanny similarity, yet so different - that promised to be an exciting and meaningful day. [1443] They walked briskly along 47th Street towards the Roman Theater. The morning wind was biting, and the streets of Broadway and its spiderweb-like alleys were deserted. A man in a brown coat loitered in front of the closed glass doors of the lobby; another leaned comfortably against the high iron grille separating the left alley from the street. In front of the theater's main entrance, they could see the short, stout Louis Pincus standing and talking to Flint. [1444] Pincus shook hands enthusiastically. \"Well, the ban is finally lifted... it's so nice to hear the news, officer,\" he said. [1445] Pincus fished out a large key ring and unlocked the lobby door. The four of them filed in. The dark-skinned manager fumbled with the lock on the inner door and then pushed it open. The darkened main hall lay before them. [1446] Ellery shuddered. \"Perhaps only the Metropolitan Opera House and Tess's grave are more lifeless than this theatre. It's the perfect place for a deceased relative.\" [1447] The officer wasn't as verbose. He grumbled and pushed his son into the darkened auditorium. \"Cut it out! You're making us nervous.\" [1448] Pincus quickly stepped forward and turned on the main power switch. Under the glare of the giant arc lights and chandeliers, the auditorium took on its familiar contours. Ellery's fanciful metaphor was not as bizarre as his father had suggested. Filthy waterproof canvas covered the rows of long seats; shadows fell on the already dusty carpet; the white plaster wall behind the empty stage was bare, forming an ugly blotch amidst the red plush. [1449] \"I apologize for the waterproof canvas,\" the officer murmured to Pincus, \"because we have to roll them up. We're going to conduct a small-scale search of the auditorium front. Flint, please bring in the two men outside. They might as well make some extra money.\" [1450] Flint hurried out and returned with the two detectives waiting outside the theatre. Under the officer's instructions, they began to pull the large coverings off the rubber chairs, revealing rows of padded seats. Ellery stood near the left aisle, pulling out a small notebook from his pocket, which he had used to sketch the theatre layout on Monday night. He pondered silently, biting his lower lip. He occasionally looked up, as if to verify the layout of the theatre. [1451] Quin quickly returned to the back of the theater, where Poulson was pacing nervously. \"Poulson, we're going to be here for hours. I didn't bring enough people. Can you help me... I need to think about something... just take up a little of your time, but it'll be very helpful to me.\" [1452] \"Of course, Officer!\" the short manager replied. \"I'll be happy to help.\" [1453] The officer coughed. \"Don't think I'm treating you like a runner or anything, old pal,\" he explained apologetically. \"But I need someone who's trained in searching for things like this... and I need some important information from a few people in the district attorney's office who are investigating other aspects of the case. Can you help me deliver a note to a man named Cronin... and bring back a package he gives you? I'm sorry to ask you to do this, Poulson, but it's too important for me to trust it to just any messenger, and\u2014I'm in a bind.\" [1454] Poulson smiled brightly. \"Don't worry about it, Officer. If you need a note, I have paper and pen in my office.\" [1455] The two returned to Poulson's office. Five minutes later, they returned to the auditorium. Poulson hurried out onto the street, holding a sealed letter. Quin watched him leave, sighed, and turned to Ellery. Ellery sat on the armrest of the chair where Field had been killed, still contemplating the pencil sketches he had made. [1456] The officer whispered something to his son. Ellery smiled and patted the older man on the back. [1457] \"Son, shall we take action?\" Quick said. \"I forgot to ask Pencier if he notified Mrs. Philip. I guess he did, or he would have said so. Where is she anyway?\" [1458] Flint was busy removing the waterproof canvas cover with two other detectives, and Quick motioned for him to come over. [1459] \"I told you to do some stretching this morning, Flint. Go up and get busy.\" [1460] \"What am I looking for today, Officer?\" the burly detective grinned. \"Because I hope my luck is better than Monday night.\" [1461] \"You're looking for a hat - a fancy, shiny hat, the kind a stylish person wears, son,\" the officer said solemnly. \"But if you see anything else, call me out loud!\" [1462] Ellery reluctantly took off his coat and slipped the pamphlet into his pocket. The officer twisted and turned to remove his overcoat, leading his son into the corridor. They began to search the audience together, slowly and methodically.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nBut in fact, in this sordid affair, it was my son Ellery who showed his cleverness. He's a smart kid.\" [2137] The officer sniffed his snuff and leaned comfortably in his armchair, Sampson and Cronin relaxed, and Juno curled up in the corner, listening intently. [2138] \"Looking back on the Field case,\" the officer began, \"I sometimes have to mention Benjamin Morgan, who was truly the most innocent victim of all. I want you to remember, Henry, whatever I say about Morgan, that's it. Don't say anything, either at work or in social circles. I've already made Tim promise to keep it secret... \"\n[2139] The two men nodded silently. The officer continued, \"Most criminal investigations begin by looking for a motive, which goes without saying. Many times, once you know the reason behind the crime, you can eliminate the suspects one by one. In this case, the motive has been unclear for a long time. Although there were some signs, such as Benjamin Morgan's secrets, they were not conclusive. Morgan had been blackmailed by Field for a long time - gentlemen, although you know Field's other social habits, you may not know some of his activities. This seems to suggest that blackmail was a possible motive - or to get rid of blackmail. However, many things could also become motives - for example, revenge by a criminal who was 'sent to prison' by Field; or by a member of his criminal gang. Field had many enemies, of course, and also many friends, but only because he held their leashes. Many people - both men and women - had a motive to kill this lawyer. That night at the Roman Theater, we had too many urgent things to consider and solve, so we didn't bother with the motive. It was always lurking behind the scenes, waiting to appear. [2140] \"But please note this: if this were blackmail - as Ellery and I eventually concluded, because this possibility seemed the greatest - then at least this can be said: Field must have had some revealing documents in his possession. We know that Morgan's documents existed. Cronin insisted that the documents he was looking for were somewhere. So we had to stay alert and look for documents - tangible evidence that might shed light on the underlying situation of the crime.\" [2141] \"Meanwhile, in terms of documents, Ellery noticed that there were many books on handwriting analysis in Field's belongings. We can conclude that, as we know, Field had blackmailed (for example, in the Morgan case) and had a suspicion of multiple cases; coupled with his strong interest in handwriting analysis, a person like him would likely forge handwriting. If so, and if it can be explained, it may mean that Field had the habit of forging the original blackmail documents. He did so, of course, to sell the forgeries and retain the originals for further blackmail. His connections with the underworld undoubtedly made him familiar with the tricks of the trade. Later, we found that this assumption was correct. At that time, we determined that blackmail was the motive for this crime. But remember, this led us astray, because any suspect could have been the victim of blackmail, and we did not know who it was. [2142] The officer frowned and adjusted his posture. [2143] \"But I handled this explanation wrongly, which shows how habits can control a person. I always start with the motive\u2026 but in the investigation, the only important, core thing that was visible was a jumbled mess of clues \u2013 or rather, the lack of clues. I mean the lost hat\u2026\n[2144] \"Unfortunately, on Monday night at the Roman Theater, we were in such a hurry to question immediately that we overlooked the significance of the lost hat. It's not that we weren't puzzled by it at first \u2013 far from it, it was one of the first things I noticed when I examined the body. As for Ellery, he noticed it when he bent down to check the body. But what could we do? We had hundreds of details to deal with \u2013 questions to ask, orders to give, contradictions and suspicious discoveries to sort out \u2013 so, as I said, we missed a major opportunity due to negligence. If we had analyzed the significance of the missing hat at the time \u2013 perhaps the case would have been solved that night. [2145] \"Well, it didn't take too long, so don't complain,\" Sampson laughed. \"It's only been nine days since the murder \u2013 what's there to complain about?\" [2146] The detective shrugged. \"There's a big difference,\" he said. \"If we had been able to deduce it at the time - well! When we finally took the time to thoroughly analyze the hat issue, the first thing we had to ask ourselves was: why would the hat be taken? There seem to be only two answers that hold up: one, the hat itself was evidence in the case; two, there was something the killer wanted in the hat, so he took the risk. It turned out that both answers were correct. The hat was evidence in the case, as the name \"Stephen Barry\" was written underneath the sweatband inside, in indelible ink; and the hat contained something the killer desperately wanted - the blackmail document. Of course, he thought it was the original at the time. [2147] \"This didn't help us much, but it was a starting point. When we ordered the theater closed and left on Monday night, despite a thorough search, we still couldn't find the missing hat. However, we also didn't know how the hat left the theater - whether it left in some mysterious way or if it was still in the theater but not found. On Thursday morning, when we returned to the theater, we finally confirmed the whereabouts of Monte Field's bothersome hat - that is, it was negative. It wasn't in the theater. Since the theater had been sealed since Monday night, it could be inferred that the hat was taken away on the night of the crime. [2148] \"And everyone left the theater on Monday night with only one hat on. Therefore, based on the second search, we can conclude that someone left the theater with or wearing Monte Field's hat that night, so he must have left his own hat in the theater. [2149] \"Unless he and the other audience members were allowed to leave and slipped away, he couldn't have taken the hat out of the theater; because all the exits were guarded or locked at that time. The left inner alley had Jesse Lynch and Eleanor Libby blocking it, followed by usher John Tice watching, and then one of our police officers guarding. There was no exit on the right, so the hat couldn't be taken from there. [2150] \"Following this line of thought - because Field's hat was a top hat, and no one leaves a theater wearing a top hat without a full evening gown - we observed this very carefully - so whoever took the\n[2151] \"It seems very logical,\" Sampson admitted, \"although it is quite complex.\" [2152] \"You have no idea how complicated this is,\" the officer said solemnly, \"because we must remember other possibilities - for example, the person who took Field's hat is not the killer, but an accomplice. Let's continue. [2153] \"The next question we ask ourselves is: What happened to the high top hat left by the murderer in the theater? How did he dispose of it? Where did he put it?... I can tell you, it's a puzzle. We searched the place up and down. Yes, we found some hats in the backstage, and Mrs. Philip, the custodian, confirmed that they were personal belongings of the actors, but none of them belonged to them privately. So, where could the high top hat left by the murderer be? Ellery hit the truth with his sharp mind. He muttered to himself: 'The murderer's hat must be here. We didn't find any unusual or unremarkable high top hats, so the high top hats we searched must not be unremarkable.' That's the crux of the matter, isn't it? Simple to the point of absurdity, yet I didn't think of it.\" [2154] \"What kind of hat is this? - An ordinary hat in an ordinary place, so unremarkable that no one questioned it. At the Roman Theater, all costumes were rented from Le Brun, so the answer is simple: it's a rented hat for the performance. Where would these hats be? Either in the men's dressing room or in the wardrobe room where all the actors' costumes are stored. When Ellery reasoned this out, he took Mrs. Philip to the backstage, checked every hat in the men's dressing room and the wardrobe room. Every single hat there \u2013 all of them accounted for, none missing \u2013 was a prop hat with Le Brun's label on the lining. And Field's hat, we had all confirmed, was a \"Brown Brothers\" derby, not among the props, and not anywhere backstage.\" [2155] \"Since no one left the theater wearing more than one hat on Monday night, and since Field's hat was undoubtedly taken the same evening, it can be concluded that the murderer's own top hat must have been in the Roman Theater during the closure period and was still there when we conducted our second search. Now, the hats left in the theater are all props. Therefore, the next inference is that the murderer left wearing Field's hat, so his own hat must have been one of the props in the backstage. So, to reiterate, these prop hats are the only actual top hats that exist.\" [2156] \"In other words - one of the props backstage belonged to the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2157] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2158] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2159] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2160] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2161] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2162] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2163] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2164] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2165] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2166] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2167] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2168] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2169] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2170] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2171] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2172] the person who left the theater on Monday night w