earing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2193] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2194] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2195] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2196] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2197] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2198]\n[2157] \"If this person is the killer \u2013 it couldn't be anyone else \u2013 then our search area is greatly reduced. He could only be one of the male actors wearing evening attire who left, or someone closely associated with the theater and dressed similarly. This person would first need to have a prop hat that must be left behind; secondly, have access to the costume storage room and dressing room without being detected; and finally, have the opportunity to leave his prop hat in the costume storage room and dressing room. [2158] \"Now let's analyze the possibility of the second scenario - the killer is closely related to the theater but not an actor.\" The policeman paused, taking a deep breath of his cherished snuff, \"The backstage staff can be ruled out as none of them wore evening gowns, which are necessary to take away Field's high hat. Similarly, the cashier, usher, doorman, and other minor staff can also be ruled out. The promoter Harry Nelson was dressed in ordinary civilian clothes. Manager Poulson wore a full suit, yes, but I measured his hat size and found it to be six and three-quarters - an unusually small size. He couldn't wear Field's hat, which was seven and three-quarters. We left the theater before him, that's right. But on my way out, I specifically instructed Thomas Wiley to search Poulson as thoroughly as he would search others. Earlier that evening, I checked Poulson's hat in his office out of a sense of responsibility and found it to be a round top hat. Wiley later reported that Poulson left with this top hat, without taking any other hats. Now - if Poulson were the person we were looking for, he might have left with Field's hat, although it was too big for him to wear, but he could have carried it. However, he left with a top hat, so there is no doubt that he could not have taken Field's hat, as the door was locked when he left the theater, and no one - I can guarantee with my staff on duty - entered the theater until Thursday morning when I opened the door myself. Theoretically, anyone who could hide Field's hat in the theater, such as Poulson or any other employee, could be the killer. But this last assumption was refuted by the report of our official building expert, Edmund Crow. He clearly stated that there are no secret hiding places in the Roman Theater. [2159] \"Having eliminated Parelli, Nelsen, and the staff, there was only the possibility of the actors left. I'll leave it to you to guess how we narrowed down the field to Barry. The most intriguing part of this case, in fact, was a series of surprising and intricate deductions that led to the truth. I should say 'we' \u2014 but really, I should say 'Eliot'\u2026\"\n[2160] \"As a detective, you are quite the bashful one,\" Crooning chuckled, \"Oh, this is more thrilling than a detective story. I should be working now, but my boss seems equally interested \u2013 go on, officer!\" [2161] Quinn smiled and continued speaking. [2162] \"The fact that the killer's identity was traced to an actor,\" he continued, \"answered a question that may have occurred to you and has been bothering us from the start. At first, we didn't understand why the theater was chosen as the meeting place for the secret deal. You stop to think, and in general, the theater has many inconveniences. Just one thing: to ensure privacy, you have to buy several extra tickets around the seats to make them empty. Compared to other more convenient meeting places, choosing such a chaotic place is simply ridiculous! The theater is mostly dark and quiet, which is unsettling. Any unexpected sound or conversation would be noticed. The audience is also a potential danger \u2013 they could be recognized. However, when you realize that Barry is an actor, all these problems are solved. From his perspective, the theater is ideal \u2013 because when the victim is found dead in the front row of the stalls, who would suspect the actor as the killer? Of course, Field consented to this arrangement, not realizing what Barry was thinking, and not realizing that he was conspiring in his own death. You must remember, he dealt with dangerous people all the time, even if he had some suspicions, he thought he could handle it. This might have made him a little too confident \u2013 of course, we'll never know. [2163] \"Let's go back to Ellery - my favorite topic,\" the detective continued, chuckling as usual, \"Besides these deductions about the hat - actually, before these deductions appeared - Ellery had noticed suspicious signs at the Evans-Pope house during their meeting. It was clear that during the intermission, Field had approached Frances Evans-Pope in the alley not just for flirting. In Ellery's view, there was some kind of connection between these two distant individuals. However, this did not mean that Frances had to know about this connection. She was absolutely certain that she had never heard of or seen Field before. We have no reason to suspect her, but we have ample reason to believe her.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nNext to him sat a young and attractive girl, dressed in a simple evening gown, wearing a shawl. She was looking up at a handsome young man in evening dress, who leaned over her chair and spoke to her in a low voice. Two other ladies were leaning forward, listening intently. [455] The chubby man sat far away from the others. As soon as Inspector Quinn walked in, he stood up and looked at the inspector with a questioning expression. The small group fell silent, their faces serious as they looked at Inspector Quinn. [456] Pincus coughed disdainfully and, with the police officer following him, sidestepped the carpet to the corner. He felt very uncomfortable surrounded by people of high status and fame. He shifted his weight and gave the officer a desperate glance. [457] Quinn walked up to the desk and faced the group. Johnson saw him wave and quickly walked over to his side. [458] \"Johnson, who are the three extra people?\" he asked in a voice that only the two of them could hear. [459] \"The old man is Morgan,\" Johnson whispered. \"The girl sitting next to him is the one I told you to find. I found her and the young man, as well as the other two women, together in the lobby. They seemed very close. When I told her your message, she looked a bit tense. But she still got up, like a person of high status, and followed me here - with the other three people as well. I don't know if you're happy to see them, officer\u2026\"\n[460] Quinn nodded. \"Did you hear what they were saying?\" he asked in a low voice. [461] \"Nothing, officer. The old man didn't seem to recognize them. The others just seemed curious about why you wanted to see her.\" [462] Quinn gestured for Johnson to move to a nearby corner. Then, turning to the crowd waiting for him, he said in a friendly tone, \"I've only called you two here to chat. Since the others are here too, they can wait here. But now, I would like to talk to this gentleman first. I have a few things to discuss with him.\" He nodded towards the villain. Johnnie stood there angrily, unmoving. [463] The group of people talked excitedly for a moment before leaving. Johnson closed the door behind them. [464] Quinn quickly turned to face Johnnie. [465] \"Bring that scoundrel over here!\" he barked at the police officer. Then, sitting in Pouzel's chair, he clasped his fingertips together. The villain was roughly pulled up by the police officer, walked across the carpet, and pushed to the front of the table. [466] \"Hey, Parsons,\" Officer Quinn says menacingly, \"I've brought you here to where I want you. Now we can chat, no interruptions. Understood?\" [467] \"The Reverend\" says nothing, his eyes filled with distrust. [468] \"So, you don't want to say anything, huh, Johnnie? How long do you think you can keep this up?\" [469] \"I've already said I know nothing, and I won't say anything until I see my lawyer,\" \"The Reverend\" says sternly. [470] \"Your lawyer? Ah, Parsons, who is your lawyer?\" Officer Quinn asks innocently. [471] \"The Priest\" bit his lip and remained silent. [472] \"Johnson, the detective,\" Quinn turned to Johnson and asked, \"you were involved in the Babylon armed robbery case, right?\" [473] \"Yes, sir,\" the agent replied. [474] \"Well, Johnny,\" Quinn said to the villain in a calm tone, \"you were sentenced to a year in prison. Do you remember that?\" [475] There was still silence. [476] \"And, Johnson,\" Quinn leaned back in his chair and continued, \"think, who was defending our friend at the time?\" [477] \"Field. It was...,\" Johnson shouted at the \"priest.\" [478] \"That's right. The gentleman lying motionless in our morgue. What's this now? Don't play dumb with me! You knew Monte Field's name just by mentioning his last name. Spill it out! Now!\" [479] The crook collapsed against the police officer, a look of despair in his eyes. He licked his lips and said, \"You're right, sir. But honestly, I... I had no idea about this. I haven't seen him for a month. I didn't\u2014oh, you're not going to pin this case on me, are you?\" [480] He looked at Quinn with pain. The police officer pulled him up straight. [481] \"Parsons, Parsons,\" Quinn said, \"how did you come to that conclusion? I was just looking for some leads. Of course, if you want to admit to killing him, I'll call my men, get everything straightened out, and we'll all go home and sleep. How about that?\" [482] \"No!\" the crook exclaimed, suddenly waving his arms wildly. The nearby policeman quickly grabbed his arm and twisted it behind him. \"How could you say that? I don't admit anything. I don't admit anything. I didn't see Field all night, and I don't know he's here! I admit... I have some influential friends, sir. I tell you, you can't frame me.\" [483] \"That's too bad, Johnny,\" Detective Quinn sighed. He took a pinch of snuff and said, \"Well, you didn't kill Monte Field. So, when did you get here tonight, and where's your ticket?\" [484] Johnny twisted his hat in his hand. \"Sir, I didn't say anything before because I thought you were going to pin a false charge on me and have me arrested. But I can explain when I came in and how I came in. I think I came in around 8:30, and I came in with a ticket, and this is the ticket stub to prove it,\" he carefully searched his pockets and pulled out a punched blue ticket stub. He handed it to the officer. Quinn took it, glanced at it, and put it in his pocket before asking, \"Where did you get this ticket, Johnny?\" [485] \"I\u2014my girlfriend gave it to me, sir,\" the crook answered nervously. [486] \"Ah, so it involves a lady,\" Quinn said happily. \"So, who is this young Tessi, Johnny?\" [487] \"Who... She's... Hey, sir, don't let her get involved in trouble, okay?\" Parson Johnny called out loudly, \"She's just an ordinary girl, and she doesn't know anything about this. Honestly, I...\u201d\n[488] \"What's her name?\" Detective Quinn asked sternly. [489] \"Maggie O'Connell,\" Johnny said sadly. \"She's the usher here.\" [490] Detective Quinn's eyes lit up, and he quickly exchanged a glance with Johnson. The detective turned and left the room. [491] \"So,\" said Quin, settling back into his chair comfortably. \"So, my old friend, Parson Johnny, has nothing to do with the murder of Monte Field, huh? Good, good, good! Let's see what your girlfriend has to say about it and see if she can clear your name.\" He kept his gaze fixed on the cheap black felt hat in Johnnie's hand. [492] He took the hat from the reluctant Johnnie and examined it, pulling out the lining to look inside before returning it to Johnnie. [493] \"We forgot one thing, Parson,\" he said. \"Officer, would you mind searching Mr. Kazzanelli, please?\" [494] \"The priest\" reluctantly allowed the police to search him, without resistance. \"No gun,\" said the officer succinctly, and continued to search. He reached into Johnnie's pockets and pulled out a bulging wallet. \"Want to see it, officer?\" said Johnnie. [495] Quin took it, quickly counted the money inside, and handed it back to the officer, returning it to Johnnie's pocket. [496] \"One hundred twenty-two dollars, Johnny,\" the old man murmured. \"I seem to smell the scent of Bonomo silk from these dollars. Oh, well!\" He smiled and asked, \"Any whiskey bottles?\" The police officer shook his head. \"Is there anything under his vest and shirt?\" Again, the answer was negative. Quinn remained silent until the search was complete. After the search, Johnny breathed a long sigh and relaxed. [497] \"Well, Johnny, you've had some luck tonight!\" [498] At the sound of a knock, Quinn said, \"Come in.\" The door opened, and a slim girl in a usherette uniform stood there. Quinn had already questioned her earlier. Johnson followed her in and closed the door. [499] Maggie O'Connell stood on the carpet, sadly looking at her lover, who was staring at the floor. She glanced at Quinn, then tightened her lips and spoke sternly to Johnny. \"Well, they caught you, you fool. I told you not to try to escape.\" She turned her back on Johnny and began to powder her face vigorously. [500] \"Why didn't you tell me earlier,\" Quinn said gently, \"that you got your friend Johnny a ticket?\" [501] \"Why should I tell you everything, sir?\" she answered rudely. \"And why should I say? Johnny has nothing to do with this.\" [502] \"We'll talk about that later,\" Detective Quinn said, playing with his snuffbox. \"Maggie, what I want to know now is whether you remembered anything after I told you. Did you ever lead Monte Field, the deceased, to his seat in the row? During the performance, did you stand at the front of the left aisle the whole time, Maggie?\" [503] \"Of course, sir. Who said otherwise?\" The girl became agitated. Quinn looked at her trembling fingers, and they stopped moving. [504] \"What do you mean?\" she asked. [505] \"I mean, you told me earlier that you were in your usual spot at the beginning of the show; you led many people to their seats; you don't remember if you ever led Monte Field, the deceased, to his seat; during the performance, you stood at the front of the left aisle the whole time. Is that right, Maggie?\" [506] \"Shut up, Maggie,\" Johnny interrupted unexpectedly, \"Don't make things worse. Either way, he'll find out we sat together, and he'll suspect you. You don't know this guy. Fess up, Maggie!\" [507] \"Well!\" Quinn said, looking at the villain and then at the girl. \"Parsons, you're getting on in years and wiser. I didn't hear you wrong, did I? You two sat together? When? Why? How long were you together?\" [508] Maggie O'Connell's face turned red and then white. She glared at Johnny and then turned to face Detective Quinn. \"I think I'll just tell you everything I know,\" she said, annoyed, \"This idiot is just a coward. I'll tell you everything I know, sir. If you don't tell that bastard manager, God will bless you.\" [509] \"I see,\" Quinn said, lowering his eyebrows. \"Young lady, if you had told me these things earlier, I could have saved a lot of time and trouble. Did you stand up during the entire second act?\" [510] \"Well, I stood up a few times, I think,\" she answered cautiously, \"But everything was normal, and the manager wasn't around, so I sat back down.\" [511] \"Did you notice anyone sitting next to Field when you stood up?\" [512] \"No, sir. I didn't know he was there. No... no, I didn't look over there, I think.\" [513] \"So, I guess,\" Quinn continued coldly, \"during the second act, you don't remember bringing anyone to the last row, the last seat next to you?\" [514] \"No, sir. I didn't know he was there. No... no, I didn't look over there, I think.\" [515] \"So, I guess,\" Quinn continued coldly, \"during the second act, you don't remember bringing anyone to the last row, the last seat next to you?\" [516] \"I don't remember, sir... uh, I know I shouldn't have done that, but I didn't notice anything out of the ordinary all night.\" As the questions escalated, she became increasingly tense. She glanced at the \"priest\" again, but he was staring at the floor. [517] \"You've been very helpful, young lady,\" Quinn said, suddenly standing up. \"You can go now.\" [518] Just as she turned to leave, the villain glanced at her innocently, stepping over the carpet to follow her. Quinn gestured to the police officer, and the \"priest\" was dragged back to his original position. [519] \"Don't rush, Johnny,\" Quinn said coldly. \"O'Connell! Turn around, girl, and try to look indifferent.\" The girl turned around, attempting to appear uninterested. \"I won't mention anything to Mr. Ponzel right now, but I advise you to be careful and mind your words when talking to your superiors. Go on, and if I hear any more trouble from you, only God can help you.\" [520] She chuckled, swaying out of the office. [521] Quin quickly turned to the officer. \"Arrest him,\" Quin pointed to the villain, his voice stern, \"Take him back to the police headquarters.\" [522] The officer saluted. The handcuffs flashed, and with a click, the \"priest\" stared blankly at the handcuffs on his wrists. Before he could say anything, he was pushed out of the office. [523] Quin waved his hand dismissively and sat down in the leather chair, taking a pinch of snuff, and then spoke to Johnson in a completely different tone: \"Could you please, Johnson, bring Mr. Morgan in?\" [524] Benjamin Morgan strode confidently into Quin's temporary office, but could not fully hide his inexplicable panic. He spoke in a pleasant, deep baritone, \"Ah, sir, I'm here.\" He sat down in a chair, a look of satisfaction on his face, as if he had just sat down in a club meeting room after a long day. Detective Quin was not fooled by his appearance. He stared intently at Morgan, making the already gray-haired, overweight Morgan feel uneasy. [525] \"I'm Quin, Mr. Morgan,\" he said in a friendly tone, \"Richard Quin, Detective.\" [526] \"I guess so,\" Morgan stood up and shook hands with Quinn, \"I assume you know who I am, officer? Several years ago, I appeared in front of you more than once in the criminal court. There was a case, do you still remember? I was Mary Duluth's defense lawyer, she was on trial for murder\u2026\"\n[527] \"Yes, I remember!\" Quinn exclaimed, \"I was wondering where I had seen you before. If I'm not mistaken, you helped her clear her name. You did a great job, Morgan - very, very nice. It's you, isn't it? Good, good!\" [528] Morgan smiled. \"That was a good job. I admit it,\" he said, \"But, I'm afraid that's all in the past, sir. You know, I don't defend people in criminal court anymore.\" [529] \"Stopped?\" Quinn took a pinch of snuff, \"I didn't know. What happened?\" [530] Morgan remained silent. After a while, he crossed his legs and said, \"There's a big problem. Can I smoke?\" He suddenly asked. After getting Quinn's consent, he lit a thick cigar and was enveloped in the smoke. [531] For a while, neither of them spoke. Morgan seemed to notice that Quinn was scrutinizing him, so he kept changing his posture, sometimes crossing his legs and sometimes putting them down, avoiding Quinn's gaze. The old man seemed to be pondering, with his head hanging on his chest. [532] This silence was tense and awkward. The only sound in the room was the ticking of the grandfather clock in the corner. Suddenly, a voice could be heard from somewhere in the theater, raising its pitch, becoming a shout or a protest, and then falling silent. [533] \"Well, officer\u2026\" Morgan coughed. He was enveloped in the smoke of the cigar, his voice was a bit shrill and tense. \"What's this\u2026 a fatigue interrogation?\" [534] Quinn looked up, surprised. \"What? What did you say, Mr. Morgan? I think I was distracted. I keep bringing up that\u2026 Oh, I'm getting old!\" [535] \"Mr. Morgan,\" Quinn asked him in his habitual jumping conversation, \"do you know why I asked you to stay and chat with me?\" [536] \"Ah, I don't know, sir. I suppose it's related to the case that happened tonight. But what does that have to do with me? I have to admit, I don't know what you're getting at.\" Morgan took a deep drag on his cigar. [537] \"Perhaps, Mr. Morgan, you'll find out soon,\" Quinn said, leaning back on the table. \"Someone was killed here tonight \u2013 it wasn't an accident, I can assure you \u2013 the victim was a man named Monte Field.\" [538] Quinn announced this with a calm tone, but it left Morgan stunned. He jumped up from his chair, his eyes wide, his hands trembling, his breath heavy, and his cigar fell to the ground. Quinn looked grim at him. [539] \"Monte \u2013 Field!\" Morgan exclaimed, sounding terrified. He stared at Quinn's face, then sat down heavily on the chair, his whole body slumping. [540] \"Pick up your cigar, Mr. Morgan,\" Quinn said. \"Mr. Pensler was so kind to lend me his office, I can't let his generosity go to waste.\" Morgan mechanically bent down and picked up the cigar. [541] Quine thought to himself, \"Either my friend is the world's best actor, or he's genuinely shocked out of his wits.\" He stood up straight. \"Alright, Mr. Morgan, pull yourself together. Why does Field's death affect you so much?\" [542] \"But... but, old chap! Monte Field... oh, my God!\" He burst out laughing\u2014a mad cackle that made Quine sit up straight with alarm. The laughter continued, and Morgan's body shook with hysteria. Quine, the detective, knew this symptom well, so he slapped Morgan and grabbed his collar, lifting him up. [543] \"Don't get carried away, Morgan!\" Quine barked. This stern voice worked. Morgan stopped laughing, looked at Quine blankly, and sat back down\u2014his body still trembling, but his demeanor now normal. [544] \"I... I'm really sorry, officer,\" he mumbled, wiping his face with a handkerchief. \"It's... it's just shocking.\" [545] \"Clearly,\" Quine said coldly, \"even if the earth opened up beneath your feet, you couldn't be more surprised. Now, Morgan, tell me what's going on.\" [546] Morgan continued wiping the sweat from his face. His cheeks were red, and he trembled, hesitatingly biting his lips. [547] \"Alright, sir,\" he finally said, \"what do you want to know?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf Stephen Barry and Field knew each other, and Frances was unaware, the possible link might be Stephen Barry. For example, if Field had an appointment with the actor on Monday evening and suddenly saw Frances, he might have dared to approach her, especially if the topic between him and Barry was related to her. As for why she recognized her - thousands of people who read the daily newspaper are familiar with her face - she is a young socialite who is often photographed. Field, with his comprehensive business methods, naturally knew her appearance and appearance... But let's go back to this triangle relationship - Field, Frances, Barry - I will explain in detail later. Through photos and other news channels, everyone knows that Barry is engaged to Frances and is her fianc\u00e9. You must have realized that, in this case, among the actors, no one else can answer this question: why did Field approach Frances? [2164] \"There is another puzzling factor about Frances - her purse was found in Field's clothes - a reasonable explanation is that, when the inebriated lawyer approached her, she dropped it out of nervousness. Later, Jesse Lynch's testimony also corroborated this, as his testimony suggested that he saw Field pick up Frances' purse. Poor girl - I feel sorry for her.\" The officer sighed. [2165] \"Let's go back to the hat\u2014you'll notice we've always been mentioning that damn hat.\" Quin paused, then continued, \"I've never seen a case where a single factor has played a part in every aspect of an investigation\u2026Now, pay attention to this: out of all the cast members, Barry was the only one who left the Roman Theater on Monday night wearing a tuxedo and a top hat. On Monday night, Ellery noticed as people were leaving the theater that everyone else in the cast was dressed in casual attire; in fact, later, when he was in Pencroft's office, he mentioned this to Sampson and me, but we didn't fully realize its significance at the time\u2026Therefore, Barry was the only cast member who could have taken Field's hat. By using Ellery's reasoning about the hat, we can now cut through the fog and conclude that Barry is the suspect. [2166] \"The next step we need to do is to see the performance ourselves. That evening - Thursday, Ellery made a decisive inference. You'll understand why. We wanted to see if Barry had time to commit the crime during the second act to confirm our conclusion. It was surprising that out of all the cast members, only Barry had the time. He left the stage at 9:20 - he went on stage, and then left almost immediately - and didn't return until 9:50, staying on stage until the act ended. This was an undeniable part of the fixed schedule. The other actors were either on stage throughout or had very short intervals between going on and off stage. This means that on Thursday night, which was five days ago, we solved the mystery - the whole case took nine days to solve, but figuring out the identity of the killer and bringing him to justice were two different things. You'll understand in a moment.\" [2167] \"The killer wouldn't be able to enter until around 9:30, which explains why the edges torn off of LL32 left and LL30 left are not consistent. You know, Field and Barry had to come in at different times. Field wouldn't be able to come in with Barry, or even be too obviously late \u2013 it was too important for Barry to keep the secret, and Field understood, or he thought he understood the necessity of adhering to the rules of secrecy.\" [2168] \"When we determined Barry to be a suspect on Thursday evening, we decided to subtly interrogate the other members of the cast and the staff behind the scenes. Of course, we wanted to find out if anyone had truly seen Barry leave or return. Coincidentally, no one had. Everyone was either busy with the performance, changing costumes, or working backstage. This investigation was conducted after the show ended that night and Barry had left the theater. This approach turned out to be quite effective. [2169] \"We borrowed a seating chart from Poulson. With this chart, along with a quick check of the left aisle after the second act on Thursday night, and the layout of the dressing rooms on stage \u2013 we can figure out how the killer did it.\" [2170] Sampson was visibly excited. \"I've been wrestling with this for quite some time,\" he admitted, \"after all, Field isn't a fool. This Barry must be a genius, Kwai, how did he do it?\" [2171] \"Once the mystery is unraveled, any riddle is simple,\" the detective answered. \"After 9:20, Barry had free time, so he quickly went back to the dressing room, did a thorough disguise on his face, put on a cloak, and a high hat, which was part of his costume - you all remember, he was wearing evening dress - and then slipped out of the dressing room and into the alley. Of course, you don't know the layout of the theater. There are many floor levels facing the left alley on the backstage side of the theater, which are dressing rooms. Barry's dressing room was on the ground floor, with a door facing the alley. There is an iron staircase leading down to the sidewalk. [2172] \"He left the dressing room through this door, slipped through the dark alley when the side door was closed during the second act. He sneaked out onto the street when no one was guarding the end of the alley - luckily for him, Jesse Lynch and his 'girl' hadn't arrived yet. Then he walked boldly into the theater as if he were late. He showed his ticket at the door - LL30 left, wrapped in a cloak, of course disguised very well. He entered the theater and deliberately threw away the stub. This was a wise move on his part, as he thought that if they found the stub there, it would only arouse suspicion in a certain audience member, not on the stage. Moreover, if his plan failed, he would be searched thoroughly later, and the stub found on him would be irrefutable evidence. Ultimately, he thought his actions would not only divert attention but also protect him. [2173] \"But how did he find his seat without a usher? If there had been an usher, he would have been seen. \"The detective answered, \"He didn't intend to avoid the usher. Since the performance was going on, the theater was dark, and he naturally hoped that the usher hadn't arrived yet and sat in the last row closest to the door. Even if the usher arrived before him and led him to his seat, with his disguise and the darkness of the theater, he wouldn't be recognized. Therefore, in the worst case, others would only remember that a man came in during the second act of the performance, and even his general outline would be difficult to describe. Coincidentally, no one approached him because Maggie O'Connell was luckily sitting next to her sweetheart. So he could sit next to Field without being noticed.\" [2174] \"But how did he find his seat without an usher? If there had been an usher, he would have been seen.\" Croaning disbelievingly. [2175] \"He had no intention of avoiding the usher,\" the officer replied, \"since it was during the performance and the theater was dimly lit, he naturally hoped the usher had not yet arrived and was seated in the back row closest to the entrance. However, even if the usher arrived before him and led him to his seat, due to his good disguise and the dark theater, he would not be recognized. Therefore, in the worst-case scenario, others' recollections of him would be of a man who entered during the second act, with a body outline that was barely describable. Coincidentally, no one approached him because Marcie O'Connell was fortunately sitting next to her sweetheart. Thus, he was able to sit quietly next to Field without being noticed. [2176] \"Please remember, what I just said is not a deduction or investigation result. We don't have the ability to discover these facts. Barry's confession last night revealed these details... Once we knew the criminal was Barry, we could deduce the whole process... If you know the criminal, it's simple and normal. However, this is not necessary for solving the case. Does this sound like an excuse for Ellery or me? Hmmph!\" The old man forced a smile. [2177] \"He had a well-planned plan when he sat next to Field,\" the officer cleared his throat, continuing. \"He had to strictly follow the schedule and not waste a minute. Moreover, Field knew that Barry had to return, so he didn't delay. Barry told us that he thought it would be difficult to deal with Field, but it wasn't as difficult as he thought. Field was amiable and accepted Barry's suggestion, perhaps because he had drunk too much and wanted to get that large sum of money quickly. [2178] \"Barry first asked for the documents. Field cunningly demanded money before showing the documents, and Barry showed him a wallet that was obviously full of real bills. The theater was very dark, and Barry didn't take out the bills. In fact, these were the bills used in the performance on stage. Barry hinted at the bills, but he wanted to check the documents first before paying. Field was probably expecting this. We know that Barry is a successful actor, and his training on stage allows him to handle difficult situations with ease...\n[2179] \"Imagine his reaction when Field reached under his seat and pulled out his high hat. Barry confessed that at the time, Field said, 'Didn't you expect me to hide the documents here? In fact, I specifically use this hat to store your records. Look - here is your name.' He said this while flipping the hatband over! Barry saw his name written in ink on the inside of the hatband with his flashlight. [2180] \"Imagine his brain's reaction at that moment. His meticulous plan was about to be ruined by this hat. If his hat had been checked when his body was found - of course, it would have been checked - the name 'Stephen Barry' on the hatband would have been the ironclad evidence... Barry didn't have time to remove the hatband. First, he didn't have a knife - unfortunately\n[2181] \"Sensitive mind, Henry, sensitive mind,\" Quinn said gravely, \"many have gone to the gallows... just as he was about to take the hat, he realized he couldn't leave his own there. For one, his hat was a fedora \u2013 a foldable, wide-brimmed hat \u2013 but more importantly, it bore the name of the costume supplier, L\u00e9on Broun. You can see, this would immediately point to someone in the production \u2013 which was exactly what he wanted to avoid. He told me, at the time and for some time after, he thought the police could only infer that something valuable was hidden in the hat. He couldn't fathom how this line of investigation would implicate him. When I explained to him that Ellery simply made deductions based on the fact of the lost hat, he was completely astounded... Now you understand, his only fundamental flaw in the commission of the crime was not negligence or error, but the unforeseen occurrence that forced him to act, which in turn set off a chain reaction. If Barry's name hadn't been on Field's hat, I wouldn't have had any suspicions, and until today, he would have been a free man with no suspicion. The police files would have added another unsolved murder case. [2182] \"As you can imagine, these thoughts flashed through his mind in a moment, not as long as I've described. He knew what he had to do, and his plans had to be adjusted immediately due to the new developments... After Field retrieved the documents from the hat, Barry quickly checked them under the watchful gaze of the lawyer. He used the same mini flashlight, which was dimmed due to the obstruction from the two of them. The documents appeared to be well-organized and complete. However, Barry didn't spend much time on them at that moment. He lifted his head and said, \"Seems like everything's here, damn it\u2014\" in a natural tone, as if they were already reconciled enemies, and he was a magnanimous person. Field didn't understand the true meaning of this statement. Barry reached into his pocket \u2013 the flashlight was off at that time \u2013 and appeared to be nervous, taking a gulp of the premium whisky from the bottle. He then seemed to realize he was being impolite and asked Field if he would like to take a sip to celebrate the successful transaction. Seeing Barry drink from the bottle, Field no longer had any suspicions. In fact, he probably never imagined that Barry would try to eliminate him. Barry handed him the bottle...\n[2183] \"But it wasn't the same bottle. Under the cover of darkness, he took out two bottles - the one he drank from was taken out of his left back pocket, while the one he gave to Field was taken out of his right back pocket. It was simple - it was dark there, and the lawyer was already drunk, so it was even simpler. The bottle trick worked. But Barry was thorough. He had a syringe filled with poison in his pocket. If Field refused to drink, Barry was prepared to inject the needle into Field's arm or thigh. He had an injection needle given to him by a doctor years ago. Barry had suffered from neurasthenia but could not undergo normal treatment by the doctor because he had to perform with the troupe in various places. Since the syringe was old, its origin could not be traced; Barry was also prepared if Field did not drink. You see - even in such details, his plan was flawless...\"\n[2184] \"The bottle of Scotch that Field drank contained high-quality whiskey, that's true, but it was heavily laced with tetraethyl lead. The strong smell of the liquor masked the subtle ether taste of the poison; Field had taken a large gulp before realizing something was amiss - if he had realized at all. [2185] \"He handed the bottle back to Barry without a second thought, and Barry pocketed it, saying, 'I think I need to look over these documents more closely - I don't trust you, Field...' Field was by now so dull-witted that he simply nodded his head blankly, then collapsed onto his seat. Barry truly examined the documents, but all the while kept an eagle's eye on Field with the corner of his eye. After about five minutes, he saw that Field was finished - completely finished. He wasn't completely unconscious, but he was close; his face contorted, and he couldn't breathe. He seemed unable to move or cry out. Of course, in his agony, he forgot all about Barry, and soon he was completely unconscious. When he spoke his last words to Basker, it was merely the dying flicker of a flame...\n[2186] \"Barry looked at his watch. It was 9:40, he had only been with Field for ten minutes. He had to be back on stage at 9:50. He decided to wait another three minutes - this took less time than he had anticipated - to ensure that Field wouldn't make any more noise. At 9:43, Field was in agony and on the brink of death, Barry picked up Field's hat, quickly removed his own hat and tucked it under his cloak, stood up. He was familiar with the route. He stuck to the wall, carefully and inconspicuously making his way along the aisle to the back of the left box. The play was at its climax, and everyone was focused on the stage, none of them noticing him. [2187] \"Behind the box, he tore off the wig, quickly adjusted his makeup, and walked through the stage door. This door led to a narrow passage, which led to a corridor, which then turned into different areas of the stage area. His dressing room was only a few feet away from the entrance of the corridor. He slipped in, tossing his prop hat into his ordinary items, pouring the remaining poison from the bottle into the sink, and washing the bottle. He poured the remaining liquid from the syringe into the toilet, and put away the cleaned needle. If he was caught - what did it matter? He had a valid reason for having the syringe, and besides, he hadn't used it in the murder case... He was now ready to go on stage, calm, happy, and confident, but also slightly annoyed. At exactly 9:50, he was called to the stage. He walked up to the stage, and at 9:55, there was a shout from the front row of the auditorium... \"\n[2188] \"Discuss your complex plans,\" said Sampson suddenly. [2189] \"It's not as complicated as it first sounded,\" the officer replied, \"Remember, Barry is a very intelligent and accomplished young man, especially an excellent actor. Only an experienced actor could pull off a plan like this. After all, the process is simple; the hardest part is sticking to the schedule. If he was seen, he would have been disguised. The most dangerous part of his plan was \u2013 how to escape when he walked down the aisle, through the backstage entrance to the stage, while the ushers were there. He had observed the ushers while sitting next to Field. Of course, he knew that due to the nature of the performance, the ushers would be mostly faithful to their duties, and he counted on his disguise and syringe to help him through any unexpected situations. However, Maggie O'Connell did not do her job properly, so that was to his advantage. He proudly told me last night that he had prepared for emergencies... As for the backstage entrance, he knew from experience that everyone would be on stage and the technicians would be busy in their positions at that time... Remember, he planned the crime with the exact conditions in mind. If there were any dangers and uncertainties \u2013 well, isn't that the nature of an adventure? \u2013 he asked me with a smile last night; I had to admit that, if not for this, I would greatly admire his style.\" [2190] The officer uneasily shifted the topic. \"I hope I've made it clear how Barry committed the crime. As for our investigation... besides the reasoning about the hat and knowing the identity of the killer, we still know nothing about the exact details of the crime. If you remember the evidence we gathered on Thursday night, you will understand that there was nothing to go on. What we hope most is that there is some clue in the files we need to find that is related to Barry. Even then, it's not enough... so the next step, the officer sighed, \"is to find the secret compartment on the canopy of the bed in Field's apartment. This work has been done by Ellery from beginning to end. We found that Field did not have a safe, no mailbox, no other residence, no intimate neighbors or business partners, and those files were not in his office. After elimination, Ellery insisted that these files were in Field's room. You know how the search ended - completely through Ellery's genius reasoning. We found Morgan's files, discovered the material needed by Croning about the mafia activities - by the way, Tim, I keenly noticed what happened when we started the big clean-up - we finally found a bundle of miscellaneous files, including Michaels and Barry's... Tim, you will remember, Ellery deduced from handwriting analysis that we might find Barry's original files - and indeed we did. [2191] \"Mickles' case was quite interesting.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThe Quinn father and son made their way through the crowd. [1704] A boy in uniform stood behind a tidy kiosk, selling paper cups. He called out his wares in a soft, affected voice. This was Jesse Lynch, the boy who had confirmed Monte Field's request for ginger ale. [1705] Ellery walked to the back of the iron door\u2014there was a narrow space between the door and the brick wall. He noticed that the walls of the building opposite the alley were mostly six stories tall and intact. The detective bought an orange juice bottle from the boy. Jesse Lynch recognized him, and the detective greeted him warmly. [1706] People stood in groups, showing an unusual interest in their surroundings. The officer heard a woman's voice, both fearful and alluring, say, \"Someone said he was there Monday night, buying a bottle of orange juice.\" [1707] Soon, the theater rang with the sound of the bell. People who had gone out to breathe quickly returned to the front row. The officer looked around the audience before sitting down. A tall, uniformed young man stood alertly on the first step of the staircase leading to the upper seats. [1708] The second act began with an explosion on stage. The audience was breathless under the influence of the sound effects. The Quinns seemed suddenly captivated by the scene. Father and son leaned forward, tense and focused. Ellery glanced at his watch at 9:30 - the Quinns sat back, and the show continued with a roar. [1709] At 9:50, they stood up, picked up their hats and coats, and quietly left LL row, walking to the empty space behind the front row. Many people were standing - the Quinns smiled at them and quietly praised the influence of the press. Maggie O'Connell, pale and drawn, leaned against a pillar, staring blankly ahead. [1710] The Quinns noticed Pouzel the manager standing at the door of his office, smiling broadly at the full house. They approached him, gesturing for him to come inside. The officer quickly stepped into the small reception room, followed by Ellery. The smile on Pouzel's face disappeared. [1711]\"I hope you've had a good evening?\" he asked nervously. [1712]\"What do you mean by 'a good evening'?\" the old man briefly gestured, leading them through the second door and into Poulson's private office. [1713]\"Listen, Poulson,\" he said, pacing back and forth, \"do you have a floor plan of the auditorium showing every seat, number, and all the exits?\" [1714]Poulson stared into his eyes. \"I think so. Wait a moment.\" He reached into the filing cabinet, searching through the folders, and finally pulled out a theater diagram, divided into two parts - one for the orchestra pit and another for the balcony. [1715]The policeman eagerly pushed the balcony part aside, and he and Ellery leaned over the auditorium pit diagram \u2460. They studied it for a while. Quinns looked up at Poulson, who stood on the carpet, shifting his weight from one leg to the other, obviously distracted and unsure of what to do next. [1716] \"Can I take this plan, Poulson?\" the officer asked tersely. \"I'll return it to you unharmed in a few days.\" [1717] \"Of course, of course!\" Poulson said. \"Is there anything else I can do for you, officer?... I appreciate your concern in the publicity department, sir\u2014Gordon Davis is very pleased with tonight's box office. He asked me to thank you.\" [1718] \"Nothing\u2014nothing,\" the officer mumbled, folding the schematic and slipping it into his chest pocket. \"It's what you deserve... Ah, Ellery\u2014are you coming with me?... Goodnight, Poulson. Remember, not a word to anyone!\" [1719] The Quin family crept out of Poulson's office, with him still prattling on about keeping the secret. [1720] They made their way back through the rear of the auditorium, heading towards the left aisle. The officer gestured for Maggie O'Connell to join them. [1721] \"Okay.\" she whispered palely. [1722] \"Open those doors a bit wider for us to exit, O'Connell, and then forget all about this. Understand?\" the officer said sternly. [1723] She grumbled quietly, pushing open a large iron door opposite LL. The officer gave her a final warning shake of the head before slipping out, followed by Ellery - the door closing gently behind them. [1724] At eleven o'clock, the final curtain fell and a throng of audience members poured out of the open exits. Richard and Ellery Quinns re-entered the Roman Theater from the main entrance. [1725] Chapter 17: More Hats\n[1726] \"Please sit down, Tim - would you like some coffee?\" [1727] Timothy Corning was sharp-eyed, medium-sized, and had a thick, unruly mane of fiery red hair. He sat in a comfortable chair in the Quine's home, slightly embarrassed by the officer's invitation. [1728] It was Friday morning, and the officer and Ellery were dressed in colorful, romantic-looking pajamas; they had gone to bed early the night before - unusually early for them - and slept soundly; now Juno served a pot of steaming hot coffee, which he had brewed himself; everything seemed so perfect. Croning's abrupt entrance into the Quine's pleasant home - disheveled, melancholy, and openly cursing - was in stark contrast to the scene. The officer's polite protests could not stop the barrage of profanity that spewed from Croning's mouth; as for Ellery, he listened intently and attentively to the legal expert's words, as if an amateur were listening to a professional's advice. [1729] Croning then realized his surroundings and blushed. He sat down after being invited, watching Juno deftly set the table for breakfast with a nimble hand. [1730] \"Your words are shocking, but I don't think you're in the mood to apologize, Tim Corning,\" the officer chided, crossing his hands behind his neck in a Buddha-like pose. \"Should I ask you why you're upset, young man?\" [1731] \"No need,\" Cropping gruffly said, restlessly moving his feet on the carpet, \"You should have guessed. I ran into a dead end when I was looking for Field's files, damn it!\" [1732] \"Damn it, Tim - damn it, don't be afraid,\" Quinn said sadly, \"Poor Field is probably roasting his toes on a hissing coal fire in hell right now - laughing at you for blasphemy. What's the matter - what's the situation?\" [1733] Cropping grabbed the cup Juno had placed in front of him and drained the scalding coffee. \"What's the matter?\" he yelled, slamming down the cup, \"Nothing - nothing! I swear, if I don't get the files soon, I'll go crazy! Hey - unbelievable. I dare swear that only God knows where Field hid the files, waiting for someone to take them away.\" [1734] \"You seem to have a phobia about hidden files, Cropping,\" Ellery said mildly, \"People would think we still live in the reign of Charles I. There's no such thing as hidden files. You just need to know where to look.\" [1735] Cropping grinned rudely. \"Thank you very much, Mr. Quinn. It seems you're hinting at where Monty Field hid his files.\" [1736] Ellery lit a cigarette. \"Well. I accept the challenge\u2026 You don't doubt what you've said \u2013 you're sure the files exist, but they're not in Field's office\u2026 Why are you so sure that Field would keep files involving such a large gang? \" [1737] \"He must have kept them,\" Croining retorted, \"odd logic, but it works\u2026 My information confirms: Field had connections with high-ranking figures in the underworld and wrote plans related to them, which we've been trying to catch but haven't managed to encounter. Believe me; this case is too complicated to investigate thoroughly. But remember my words, Mr. Quin \u2013 Field has files he dare not destroy. Those are the files I'm looking for.\" [1738] \"Right,\" Ellery countered, \"I just want to confirm some facts. So, let me repeat: the files aren't in his office. So we have to expand the search. For example, they might be in a safe.\" [1739] \"But, Ellery,\" the officer objected, who had been listening intently to the exchange between Croining and Ellery, \"didn't I tell you this morning that Thomas had checked it out? There are no boxes in Field's safe. It's been confirmed. He has neither a mailbox to be picked up nor a private mailbox \u2013 either under his real name or an alias. [1740] \"Thomas also investigated the clubs closely associated with Field and found that the lawyer had no other residence, either fixed or temporary, apart from his apartment on 75th Street. And throughout Thomas's search, there was no sign of any possible hiding place. He thinks Field may have put the files in a package or bag and given it to a shopkeeper to keep. But there's no trace\u2026 Weli is an expert in this area, Ellery. I can assure you that your assumption is wrong.\" [1741] \"I'm doing this for Cronin's sake,\" Ellery countered, spreading his fingers on the table and blinking. \"You see, we must narrow down the search until we can say with certainty: 'It's definitely here.' The office, the safe, and the post office have been ruled out. However, we know that Feld wouldn't keep these files in an inaccessible place. I can't guarantee the file you're looking for, Cronin; it's not the same one we're looking for. We know Feld kept them in a place he could easily reach... Furthermore, we can reasonably infer that he would keep all his important secret files in the same hidden place.\" [1742] Cronin scratched his head and nodded. [1743] \"Now we have to apply basic principles, gentlemen,\" Ellery paused, emphasizing the next sentence. \"Since we have narrowed down the investigation to exclude all possible hiding places, leaving only one - the file must be there... there's no difficulty.\" [1744] \"Let me think,\" the officer interjected, suddenly becoming somber after his earlier joviality. \"Perhaps we should have searched that place more thoroughly, but we didn't.\" [1745] \"I'm sure our thinking is right,\" Ellery said firmly. \"Just as today is Friday, and tonight there will be three million households eating fish.\" [1746] Cronin looked perplexed. \"I don't quite understand, Mr. Quin. What do you mean by there being only one possible hiding place?\" [1747] \"Field's apartment, Cronin,\" Ellery said calmly, \"The file is there.\" [1748] \"But I discussed this case with the district attorney yesterday,\" Cronin objected, \"he said you'd already thoroughly searched Field's apartment and found nothing.\" [1749] \"That's correct - absolutely correct,\" Ellery said, \"we searched Field's apartment and found nothing. The problem is, Cronin, we didn't look in the right place.\" [1750] \"Well, now that you know, let's go,\" Cronin exclaimed, springing from his chair. [1751] The officer gently tapped the red-haired man's knee and pointed to the seat. \"Tim, sit down,\" he advised. \"Ellery is just indulging in one of his favorite deduction games. As for where the files are, he knows no more than you do. He's guessing\u2026in detective literature,\u201d he added with a wry smile, \"it's called 'deduction.'\" [1752] \"I suppose,\" Ellery muttered, puffing on his cigar, \"I'm being questioned again. I'm going back to Field's room to find those elusive files, but I haven't gotten the officer's approval yet.\" [1753] \"About those files\u2014\" the old man began, but was interrupted by the doorbell. Juno opened the door to let Detective Wiley and a small, shifty-looking man enter. The officer stepped in front of them before they could enter the living room. Croning looked at them, and Quinn asked, \"Thomas, is that the guy?\" [1754] \"You thought you could break in and get away with it, didn't you?\" the officer asked the newcomer, kindly but firmly. \"You're the one I've been looking for.\" [1755] The sneaky-looking young man seemed to freeze. \"Ah, you're not pulling my leg, are you?\" he stammered. [1756] The officer smiled reassuringly and led the young man out to the foyer. They whispered back and forth, and the old man nodded in agreement with every word the officer said. Croining and Eliot, sitting in the living room, saw a small note flutter by as the officer slipped it into the young man's tightly clenched hand. [1757] Quine strode back in. \"Okay, Thomas. Take care of other things and make sure our friend doesn't get into trouble...now, gentlemen...\"\n[1758] Detective Wiley bid a brief farewell and led the apprehensive stranger out of the apartment. [1759] The officer sat down. \"Before we go to Field's room, gentlemen,\" he said thoughtfully, \"I want to clarify a few things. First, based on what Benjamin Morgan told us, Field's business was law, but his main source of income was...extortion. You know that, Tim? Monte Field had drained many influential people, amounting to nearly half a million dollars. In fact, Tim, we believe the motive for murdering Field was related to his recent illicit activities. Without a doubt, the person who killed him was paid a large sum of money to keep quiet and had reached their limit. [1760] \"Tim, you and I both know that extortion mainly relies on the extortionist possessing incriminating documents about someone's unsavory past. That's why we're so sure the files are hidden somewhere...Eliot believes they're in Field's room. Let's see if we can find them. If we do, the archives you've been looking for may resurface, as Eliot pointed out earlier.\" [1761] He paused thoughtfully. \"Tim, you don't know how much I want to get Field's damn files. They're crucial to me, to unravel many mysteries that have kept us in the dark so far...\"\n[1762] \"Okay, let's get started!\" Croining exclaimed, leaping up from his chair. \"Do you know how many years I've been tracking Field for this purpose? This will be the happiest day of my life... policeman - let's go!\" [1763] However, Ellery and his father seemed unfazed. They returned to the bedroom to change clothes while Croining paced restlessly in the living room. If Croining had not been so engrossed in his own affairs, he would have noticed the light-hearted mood of the Quin family when he first entered, now shrouded in gloom. Especially the policeman seemed to be in a bad mood, restless, and hesitant to push the investigation forward. [1764] The Quin family finally dressed and went downstairs. As they got into a taxi, Ellery sighed. [1765] \"Are you afraid of being embarrassed, son?\" the old man grumbled into his coat folds. [1766] \"It's not that,\" Ellery replied, \"It's something else\u2026 The files will be found, don't worry.\" [1767] \"I sure hope you're right,\" Cromwell breathed heavily. The rest of the ride was silent until the taxi pulled up in front of the towering apartment building on 75th Street. [1768] The trio took the elevator to the fourth floor and stepped into the quiet hallway. The officer glanced around quickly before ringing the doorbell to Field's apartment. There was a rustling sound from behind the door, but no one answered. Suddenly, the door swung open, revealing a red-faced police officer with his hand nervously fidgeting in his back pocket. [1769] \"Hey, don't be scared \u2013 we won't bite you!\" the officer barked, inexplicably angry, which puzzled Cromwell. He was as jumpy as a racehorse. [1770] The uniformed officer saluted. \"I didn't realize it was you, Officer. I thought someone was casing the place,\" he said, sounding deflated. [1771] The three entered the foyer, and the elderly man quickly shut the door with his bony, pale hand. [1772] \"What's going on around here?\" Quin asked gruffly, stepping into the living room and looking around. [1773] \"Nothing, sir,\" the policeman said. \"Myself and Cassidy take four-hour shifts, and Ritter checks in occasionally to make sure everything's okay.\" [1774] \"Oh, he came by, did he?\" the old man turned around. \"Anyone want to come in here?\" [1775] \"No one was here when I was here, sir \u2013 and Cassidy wasn't here either,\" the policeman answered nervously. \"We've been on rotation since Tuesday. There hasn't been a soul around these rooms besides Ritter.\" [1776]\"Stay in the foyer for the next few hours, officer,\" the officer commanded. \"Bring a chair over and take a nap if you want\u2014but if anyone comes to cause trouble, report to us immediately.\" [1777]The policeman dragged a chair from the living room and sat with his back against the front door, crossing his arms and closing his eyes. [1778]The three of them looked grimly at the scene. The porch, though small, was cluttered with various pieces of furniture and decorations. The bookshelves were filled with seemingly unopened books; a modernist table lamp and a few carved ivory ashtrays sat on a small table; two neoclassical chairs; an unusual piece of furniture that looked half like a sideboard and half like a desk; and numerous cushions and small carpets were scattered about. The officer grimaced at the mess. [1779]\"Hey, son\u2014I think the best way to search is for us to go through everything one by one, with one person searching and the other tidying up. I'm not too optimistic about it, I'll say that upfront.\" [1780]\"The gentleman of the Wailing Wall,\" Ellery grumbled under his breath, sadness evident on his noble face. \"You and me, Cronin\u2014we won't be so pessimistic, will we?\" [1781] Cronin barked, \"I mean\u2014less talk, more action, I treat family squabbles with the utmost respect.\" [1782] Ellery admiringly gazed at him. \"You make decisions like an insect, buddy. More like an ant than a human. Poor Field lies in the morgue... Let's get to work, kids!\" [1783] They began working in front of the dozing policeman, mostly in silence. Ellery's face showed a calm anticipation, the officer displayed grimness and annoyance, and Cronin wore a face of brusqueness and indomitable spirit.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[548] \"That's good,\" Quine approved. \"So, when was the last time you saw Monty Field?\" [549] Morgan cleared his throat uneasily. \"Er... I haven't seen him in a while,\" he whispered in response. \"You see, we used to be partners - successful in legal matters. Then some things happened, and we went our separate ways. Since then, I... I haven't seen him again.\" [550] \"How long ago was that?\" [551] \"About two years and a bit.\" [552] \"Very good.\" Quinn leaned forward. \"I also want to know why you split up.\" [553] Morgan looked down at the carpet, fiddling with his cigar. \"I... uh, I guess you know what kind of person Field is. We had a disagreement about ethics, and after a small argument, we decided to go our separate ways.\" [554] \"Did you part on good terms?\" [555] \"Uh... in that situation, yes.\" [556] Quinn tapped the table. Morgan shifted uncomfortably. He clearly hadn't fully recovered from the shock. [557] \"What time did you arrive at the theater tonight, Morgan?\" the officer asked. [558] Morgan seemed surprised by the question. \"Ah\u2014around 8:15,\" he replied. [559] \"Let me see your ticket stub,\" Quinn said. [560] Morgan fumbled through his pockets before producing it and handing it to Quinn. Quinn took the ticket stub, then pulled out three more from his pockets, placing them under the table and carefully comparing them. He then returned the four ticket stubs to his pocket without expression. [561] \"So, you were sitting in the middle M2 seat, right?\" he said. \"Nice spot, Morgan. What made you want to see 'Gunplay'?\" [562] \"Ah, it's a novelty show, isn't it, officer?\" Morgan looked a bit embarrassed. \"I didn't even know I'd want to come to the theater \u2013 you know, I don't usually go to the theater \u2013 but the manager of the Roman Theater sent me a free ticket for tonight's performance, so I came.\" [563] \"Is that so?\" Quine asked bluntly. \"When did you receive the ticket?\" [564] \"Ah, I got the letter on Saturday morning, officer, delivered to my office.\" [565] \"Oh, you got a letter too, did you? You don't happen to have it on you, do you?\" [566] \"I\u2014very\u2014sure\u2014I brought it,\" he mumbled, starting to rummage through his pockets, \"Here it is! Here you go.\" [567] He handed the officer a small rectangular piece of rough-edged paper made of wrinkled securities printing paper. Officer Quinn carefully took it to the light. Through the printed lines, a clear watermark pattern could be seen. He pursed his lips and carefully placed the paper on the desk's notepad. Under Morgan's gaze, he opened the drawer above Posen's desk and searched until he found a sheet of paper. The paper was large and square, with a luxurious theater seal printed on it. Quinn placed the two papers side by side, pondered for a moment, then sighed, picked up the one Morgan handed him, and read it slowly. [568] The Roman Theater Department cordially invites Benjamin Morgan, Esq. to attend the performance of 'Gunfight' on September 24th (Monday) evening. We sincerely hope that Mr. Morgan, a leading figure in the legal profession in New York, will share his valuable opinions on the social and legal aspects of the play. Of course, we do not impose any obligation. The Roman Theater Department guarantees that Mr. Morgan can accept this invitation without any obligation. [569] (Signature) Roman Theater\n[570] Inviter: S.\n[571] The \"S\" written in ink was sloppy and almost unrecognizable. [572] Quin looked up, smiled. \"Roman Theatre is quite something, Mr. Morgan. I was wondering -\" he continued to smile, gesturing towards Johnson, who had been sitting quietly in the corner, listening to their conversation. [573] \"Call the manager, Johnson,\" Quin said, \"if that publicity man - that Bellson, or Pearson, or whoever - is here, have him come too.\" [574] After Johnson left, Quin turned back to Morgan. [575] \"Could you show me your gloves, Mr. Morgan?\" he whispered. [576] Morgan looked at him perplexed and then threw the gloves on the table in front of him. Quinn picked them up curiously. They were a pair of white silk gloves \u2014 typically worn with evening attire. Quinn pretended to be busy examining the gloves. He turned them over, closely inspecting a smudge on one fingertip, and even tried to put them on his own hand, joking with Morgan. After his examination, he returned the gloves to Morgan with a serious expression. [577] \"Ah \u2014 oh, by the way, Mr. Morgan \u2014 your hat is quite a sight. May I see it?\" [578] Morgan placed the hat on the table, still silent. Quinn picked it up nonchalantly, whistling a tune called \"Broadway,\" a few notes lower than standard pitch. He flipped the hat in his hand. It was a high-quality hat, with a lustrous silk lining and a gold stamp of the manufacturer, \"James Chalmers & Co.\" The initials \"B.M.\" were embroidered on the band. [579] Quinn grinned and put the hat on his own head. It fit perfectly. But he quickly took it off and handed it back to Morgan. [580] \"Thank you very much for allowing me to examine your gloves and hat, Mr. Morgan,\" he said as he pulled out a notepad from his pocket and jotted down a note hastily. [581] The door opened, and Johnson, Pensel, and Harry Nelson entered. Pensel hesitated before approaching, while Nelson sat in an armchair. [582] \"What can I do for you, officer?\" Pensel asked nervously, trying to ignore the dejected, silver-haired figure sitting in the chair. [583] \"Mr. Pensel,\" Quinn said calmly, \"In your Roman Theatre, how many kinds of writing paper do you have?\" [584] The manager's eyes widened. \"Just one, officer. There's one right in front of you on the table.\" [585] \"Ah \u2013\" Quinn handed Pensel the paper he had taken from Morgan. \"I want you to take a good look at this paper, Mr. Pensel. Do you know if the Roman Theatre has such paper?\" [586] The manager gave the paper a strange look. \"No, I don't think we have such paper. In fact, I'm quite sure. What does it say?\" He turned around and called out, \"Nelson! What is this?\u2014Is it your latest promotional gimmick?\" He waved the paper in front of Nelson. [587] Nelson took the paper from the manager and quickly read it. \"Ah, it seems I'll have to change careers,\" he whispered. \"This is bound to break the advertising record.\" He read it again, his face showing envy. Then, under the gaze of four reproachful eyes, he handed the paper back to Pencier. \"I'm sorry, such a brilliant idea didn't even occur to me,\" he said slowly, retreating to the corner and crossing his arms. [588] The manager turned to Quine with a puzzled expression. \"This is strange, officer. As far as I know, the Roman Theater never uses such stationery, and I'm quite certain I never authorized anyone to do such promotional work. If Nelson says he didn't do it\u2014\"\n[589] Quine carefully put the paper in his pocket. \"Alright, thank you, gentlemen,\" he said, nodding at Pencier and Nelson as they left. [590] He looked at Morgan, who was flushed with anger and embarrassment. Quine raised his hand and slammed it lightly on the table again. [591] \"What do you think of this, Mr. Morgan?\" He asked straightforwardly. [592] Morgan jumped up. \"Damn it, this is a frame-up!\" He waved his fist in front of Quinn, \"Please excuse my rudeness, I know no more about this than you do. And don't think you can scare me by searching my hat and gloves - oh, you haven't searched my underwear yet, Inspector!\" [593] \"But my dear Morgan,\" Inspector Quinn said gently, \"why do you have to make yourself so nervous? Others would think I accused you of murdering Monte Field. Sit down, calm down, old boy. I'll ask you a simple question.\" [594] Morgan slumped in the chair, wiped his forehead with a trembling hand, and whispered, \"Sorry, Inspector, I couldn't control my temper. But these unfortunate things -\" He calmed down and murmured. [595] Inspector Quinn sat there, staring at him with a puzzled look. Morgan was busy wiping his sweat with a handkerchief and puffing on a cigar. Johnson coughed disapprovingly and looked up at the ceiling. Suddenly, there was a commotion in the room, but it soon quieted down. [596] Quin's voice broke the silence. \"Well, that's all, Mr. Morgan. You can go now.\" [597] Morgan slowly rose, opened his mouth to say something, but closed it again, put on his hat, and left the room. Johnson saw the officer's gesture and lazily stood up, casually opening the door for him. Both men left. [598] The room was left with Quin alone, who immediately got to work. He pulled out four ticket stubs, the letter Morgan had given him, and the lady's rhinestone handbag he had found in the dead man's pocket. He opened the handbag again, took out its contents, and laid them on the table in front of him. There were several business cards with \"Frances Eves-Popp\" printed elegantly; two delicate handkerchiefs; a small makeup case containing foundation, blush, and lipstick; a small money pouch containing twenty-dollar bills and some coins; and a door key. Quin toyed with these items for a moment, put them back in the handbag, and put the handbag, ticket stubs, and letter back in his pocket. He stood up, slowly scanning the room, then walked over to the coat rack, picked up a top hat hanging there, and checked its lining. It had the initials \"L.P.\" printed on it, and the hat size was 6 3/4, which piqued his interest. [599] He put the hat back in its place, opened the door. [600] The four people sitting in the reception room immediately stood up, their faces expressing relief. Quin stood at the door, smiling, with his hands in his coat pockets. [601] \"It's finally your turn,\" he said. \"Come on, everyone, come into the office.\" [602] He politely stepped aside to let them go first \u2013 three ladies and a young man. They all entered the office with some excitement, the young man hurrying to pull chairs for the ladies to sit down. Four pairs of eyes eagerly looked at the elderly man standing at the door. Quein smiled, like a fatherly figure. He quickly glanced at the reception room, then closed the door, and sternly walked to the table and sat down, reaching for his snuffbox. [603] \"Alright!\" he said mildly. \"I'm very sorry to have kept you waiting so long \u2013 official business, you know. Now, let's get started. Well, right, right, I must \u2013 well, now, first of all, ladies and this gentleman, how shall we begin? I believe, young lady, although I haven't had the pleasure of hearing you introduce yourself, your name should be Frances Eves-Pope, is that right?\" [604] The girl raised her eyebrows. \"Yes, sir,\" her voice was lively and melodious, \"although I'm not sure how you know my name.\" [605] She smiled. Her smile was attractive and seductive, and her gaze was captivating. She was in her prime, graceful and charming, with brown, large eyes and a complexion like jade. She exuded vitality that revitalized Quein's spirits. [606] He smiled at her. \"Well, Miss Eves-Pope, I think for most people, your identity would be a mystery. But I'm a policeman, so I'm stronger than most people, so it's not difficult for me to know your name. And you're definitely not an unknown girl, in fact, I saw your photo in the social section of the newspaper today.\" [607] The girl smiled, a little nervous. \"Oh, so that's how it is!\" She said, \"I'm starting to get scared. Sir, what do you want with me?\" [608] \"Official business - always official business,\" Detective Quin said pitifully, \"I'm interested in anyone, I always relate it to my profession... before asking you questions, I want to ask, who are your friends?\" [609] When Detective Quin looked at the three of them, all three coughed awkwardly. Miss Francis smiled charmingly. \"I apologize, sir, please allow me to introduce my friends. These two ladies are Hilda Orangi and Eve Ellis, my good friends; this is Mr. Stephen Barry, my fianc\u00e9.\" [610] Detective Quin looked at them with a touch of surprise. \"If I'm not mistaken, you're all members of the cast of 'Gunfight', right?\" [611] All three nodded in unison. [612] Queene turned to Francis. \"I don't mean to pry, Miss Eves-Pope, but I want you to explain... why your friends are here with you?\" [613] The three actors stood up very awkwardly. Francis looked from her companions to Queene, pleading with her eyes. [614] \"I\u2014I'm sorry, officer,\" she said quickly. \"I... I've never been questioned by the police before. I'm nervous, and I want my fianc\u00e9 and these two ladies\u2014my closest friends\u2014to be here with me when you ask me questions. I didn't realize it went against your wishes...\u201d\n[615] \"I understand,\" said Queene, smiling. \"I completely understand. But, you see\u2014\" he made a gesture for them to go out first. [616] Stephen Barry leaned over the girl's chair, a challenging look in his eyes as he stared at Quinn. \"Darling, I won't leave if you need me.\" [617] \"But, Stephen, dear\u2014\" Frances helplessly sobbed. Quinn's expression remained firm, showing no signs of relenting. \"You...you should go out first. But please wait outside for me. It won't be long, right, sir?\" she asked, her eyes showing displeasure. [618] Quinn shook his head. \"Not too long.\" His attitude had changed completely, seeming to become somewhat irritable. The other three seemed to sense his change, subtly aligning themselves against him. [619] Hilda Orlandi was a tall, plump woman in her forties, still attractive despite her age, with traces of her youthful beauty on her face. Under the cold light in the room, her face lost its color. She leaned over Frances, staring at Detective Quinn with a firm tone in her voice. \"We'll wait for you outside, dear, if you feel uncomfortable or anything, just call out, and we'll come right in to help you.\" She huffed out of the room, followed by Eve Ellis, who patted Frances' hand comfortingly. \"Don't worry, Frances, we're all here for you.\" She tugged on Barry's arm and followed Hilda Orlandi out. Barry glanced back at Quinn, a mix of anger and concern in his eyes. He glared at Quinn again before slamming the door shut. [620] Quinn immediately stood up, his movements clean and his attitude cold. He stared into Frances' eyes, his hand resting on the surface of the table. \"Now, Frances Eves-Pope, Miss,\" he said succinctly, \"I'd like to talk to you about this...\u201d He reached into his pocket, pulling out a rhinestone purse like a stage magician. \"I'd like to return your purse to you.\" [621] Francis sat up a little, her gaze shifting from Detective Quinn to the sparkling handbag. Her face turned pale. \"Oh, that's... that's my evening bag!\" [622] \"Indeed, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Quinn said, \"it was found at the theater\u2014tonight.\" [623] \"Of course!\" She smiled nervously and sat back down. \"I'm such a fool! I didn't even know my bag was missing until now...\"\n[624] \"But, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Detective Quinn continued solemnly, \"where we found your bag is more important than finding the bag itself.\" [625] Francis opened her mouth wide, staring at him with fear in her eyes. \"Yes, I heard about it.\" She whispered. [626] \"Well, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Quinn coldly continued, \"your bag was found in the pocket of the deceased!\" [627] Fear filled the girl's eyes. She let out a sob and swayed on her chair, her face pale and tense. [628] Quinn leaped forward, a look of pity and concern on his face. He touched Francis's weak body just as the door burst open and Stephen Barry rushed into the room, his coat flapping behind him. Hilda Orlancy, Eve Ellis, and Johnson followed close behind. [629] \"What did you do to her, you damned detective!\" Barry shouted, shoving Quinn aside. He gently held Francis in his arms, brushing aside the strand of black hair that was in front of her face and whispering comforting words in her ear. Francis sighed and looked up at the agitated face next to her, confused. \"Stephen, I...I feel dizzy,\" she murmured, and then sank back into Stephen's embrace. [630] \"Bring some water!\" the young man exclaimed, stroking her hand. Johnson immediately handed over a flat glass cup. Barry forced a few gulps down her throat, and Francis coughed and regained her senses. The two actresses pushed Barry aside and sternly ushered the men out of the room. Quinn followed the protesting young man and the detective out the door with a submissive expression. [631] \"You're a good cop, aren't you?\" Barry sniped at Quinny. \"What did you do to her \u2013 give her a whack on the head with your usual police tactics?\" [632] \"Oh, oh, young man,\" Quinny said mildly, \"don't talk so harshly. She was just frightened.\" [633] They all stood there in silence, the tension palpable. The door opened, and the two actresses emerged, supporting Francis. Barry rushed to her side, stroking her hand and whispering, \"Do you feel better now, dear?\" [634] \"Stephen \u2013 take me \u2013 home,\" she gasped, leaning on Barry. [635] Quinny stepped aside, letting them pass. He watched them slowly make their way to the front door, accompanied by a small crowd of spectators. [636] Chapter Six: The District Attorney Becomes a Biographer\n[637] Detective Richard Quinn was a unique man. Although small in stature, he was remarkably sturdy; his head was full of white hair, and his face was etched with lines that spoke of his rich life experiences.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe could have been a corporate executive or a night watchman, depending on his whim. Of course, with the right clothes, his unremarkable build could pass for any profession. [638] This adaptability was also evident in his demeanor. No one could truly understand him. He was a source of wonder for his colleagues, his enemies, and those wretches he handed over to the legal system. He could be any kind of personality, from mild-mannered to arrogant, from paternal to obstinate, depending on his whim. [639] However, deep down, as some more emotional people would argue, Quinn had a \"heart of gold.\" He had no malice in his heart, was warm-hearted, and was not affected by the cruel world. For those who dealt with him for business or official matters, he was a chameleon, and indeed he was. He always showed different aspects of his personality, as he found it good: people never understood him, never knew what he was going to say or do, so they always had a bit of fear of him. [640] At this moment, he returned to Pensel's office alone, closed the door, paused the investigation, and returned to his true self. At this moment, his face was wrinkled - physically old, but mentally wise. The image of the frightened, terrified girl kept flashing in his mind. Seeing her frail, terrified face made him want to escape. Francis Eves-Pope embodied everything an aging person would want their daughter to have. Seeing her fianc\u00e9 desperately defending her made him feel ashamed. [641] He sighed, reached for his snuff box, and took a deep drag. Although he was usually thrifty, he was willing to spend money on this. [642] There was a sudden knock at the door, and he immediately changed into an officer's demeanor - sitting at the desk, pondering the case. In fact, he hoped that Ellery would come in. [643] He said warmly, \"Come in.\" The door opened, and a thin, bright-eyed man in a heavy coat and a wool scarf walked in. [644] \"Henry!\" the officer called, standing up. \"I thought the doctor told you to stay in bed!\" [645] District Attorney Henry Sampson winked at Quinn and sat down in an armchair. [646] \"Doctor,\" he lectured, \"Doctors are so annoying. Now, tell me about the case. How's it going?\" [647] He groaned, carefully feeling his throat. Detective Queen sat down. [648] \"For an adult, Henry,\" he said firmly, \"you're the most uncooperative patient I've ever seen. You'll get pneumonia if you're not careful.\" [649] \"Well,\" the district attorney grinned, \"I have insurance, so I should worry... Oh, you haven't answered my question yet.\" [650] \"Oh, yes,\" Queen grumbled, \"your question. The case, you ask? It's going nowhere, my dear Henry. Is that satisfactory for you?\" [651] \"Please be more detailed,\" Henry said, \"remember, I'm a patient, my head is still buzzing.\" [652] \"Henry,\" Quine said, leaning forward solemnly, \"I'll tell you, this case is the most difficult one we've ever encountered in our department...Your head is buzzing, isn't it? I don't even want to talk about my head.\" [653] Henry frowned, looking at Detective Quine. \"If it's really as you say\u2014I think\u2014this is not a good time. The election is coming soon, an unsolved murder case, if it falls into the hands of the opposition... \"\n[654] \"Well, that's just one aspect,\" Quine whispered, \"I haven't considered the case from the election perspective, Henry. Someone was killed\u2014to be honest, I have no idea who killed him or how he was killed.\" [655] \"I accept your kind criticism, officer,\" Henry's tone lightened, \"but if you knew what I heard just a few minutes ago\u2014on the phone...\"\n[656] \"Wait a moment, my dear Watson\u2014as Ellery often says,\" Quin chuckled\u2014his sudden change in demeanor was typical of him, \"I bet I know what happened. You were at home, probably in bed. The phone rang, and then you heard complaints, protests, grumbling, and various sounds a person makes when agitated. The voice said: 'I can't believe I'm being treated like a criminal, being detained by the police. I want Quin to be severely punished! He's a serious threat to personal freedom!' and so on, right?\" [657] \"My dear fellow!\" Sampson smiled. [658] \"This gentleman, the one who complained to you,\u201d the officer continued, \u201cis a short, chubby man with gold-rimmed glasses, speaks like a woman, and has a very unpleasant voice. He also showed great concern for 'a very good friend, District Attorney Henry,' which is touching, isn't it?\" [659] Sampson sat there, staring at Quin. Then, a smile spread across his eager face. [660] \"It's shocking, my dear Sherlock!\" he whispered, \"Since you know so much about my friend, perhaps you can easily tell me his name?\" [661] \"Ah - is it this guy, right?\" Quine said, blushing, \"I - Ellery, my child! Nice to see you.\" [662] Ellery entered the room, shaking Sampson's hand warmly. Sampson greeted him enthusiastically, having worked with him for a long time. Ellery inquired about the District Attorney's condition and then set a large jar of coffee and a paper bag on the table, which could not help but make one think of French pastries inside. [663] \"Come on, gentlemen, the big search is finally over, done, even though the yield was small, the hardworking investigators now deserve a nightcap,\" he chuckled, patting his father's shoulder affectionately. [664] \"But, Ellery!\" Quine exclaimed happily, \"this is a great surprise! Henry, do you want to join us and celebrate a little?\" [665] \"I don't know what you're celebrating, but count me in,\" Sampson said. The three then enjoyed their meal together. [666] \"How's it going over there, Ellery?\" the old man asked, sipping his coffee contentedly. [667] \"Gods can go without eating or drinking,\" Ellery mumbled while eating a cream puff, \"I'm not all-powerful. So, tell me first what happened in your temporary interrogation room... but I can tell you something you don't know. Mr. Libby - the owner of the ice cream shop - confirmed what Jesse Lynch said about ginger ale, and Miss Eleanor Libby confirmed everything that happened in the alley.\" [668] Quinn wiped his mouth with a large handkerchief and said, \"Well, whatever, let Plautti check the ginger ale to make sure everything is fine. As for me, I just asked a few people, and now there's nothing else to do.\" [669] \"Thanks,\" Ellery said calmly, \"your statement is perfect! Have you told the district attorney about the events of this chaotic night?\" [670] \"Gentlemen,\" Sampson said, putting down his cup, \"I'll tell you what I've learned. About half an hour ago, a very good friend of mine called me - he happens to have some background - and clearly told me that someone was killed during tonight's performance. He accused you of violating the law, saying that Detective Richard Quinn rushed into the theater like a whirlwind, followed by the little whirlwinds, and made everyone wait for an hour - completely out of order, absolutely unforgivable. He also testified that Detective Quinn accused him of a crime and even ordered the overbearing police to search him, his wife, and daughter before they left the theater. [671] \"That's all the information my informant provided \u2013 the rest was just insults and complaints, not relevant to the case. The other thing is that Mr. Wiley just told me the identity of the victim. And, gentlemen, that's the most interesting part of the whole incident.\" [672] \"You know as much about this case as I do, Ellery,\" Quine grumbled, \"if not more, because I think you're quite familiar with Mr. Field's business activities... Ellery, what happened during the search outside?\" [673] Ellery comfortably crossed his legs. \"You probably guessed it, too, that the search of the audience didn't yield any results. We didn't find anything out of the ordinary. No one looked suspicious, and no one confessed. So, the search was a failure.\" [674] \"Of course, of course,\" Quine said, \"the person who planned this murder was very clever. I suppose you didn't see any suspicious extra hats?\" [675] \"This, Father,\" Ellery said, \"is why I'm here in the hall. No, there were no suspicious hats.\" [676] \"Is everything over over there?\" [677] \"I just finished buying pastries on the street,\" Ellery said, \"there was nothing going on over there either. They just had to make the angry audience line up and go out. Now, everyone's out, including the audience upstairs, the theater staff, and the cast\u2026 These actors are quite peculiar, they spend the whole night pretending to be God, pretending to be all-powerful; then suddenly they find themselves reduced to mere mortals, subject to the same troubles and diseases as everyone else. By the way, Willie searched the five people who left the office. The young lady has a very nice car. I guess it's Miss Evans-Pope and her friends\u2026 I don't know, but you probably forgot about them by now.\" [678] \"So, we're out of ideas, are we?\" Quine grumbled, \"The case is this.\" [679] \"That's it,\" Quine summarized after succinctly recounting what had happened in the small office, \"Now, Henry, you must have something to tell us about Monte Field. We know he's a very slick guy \u2014 but that's all we know about him.\" [680] \"You're being polite,\" Henry said harshly, \"I can recite his life story from memory. It seems you're in trouble now, and I think his past experiences might provide you with some clues.\" [681] \"Field was first investigated by our department during my predecessor's tenure,\" Henry continued. \"We suspected him of being involved in a scam involving an illegal syndicate. Assistant District Attorney Crowning was in charge of the case, but he couldn't catch Field on anything. Field's actions were very discreet. All we knew was what a snitch kicked out of the mob told us, but we couldn't verify the truth of it. Of course, Crowning never let Field know we suspected him. The case eventually cooled down, although Crowning persisted in his investigation, only to find nothing every time he thought he had something. Oh, you're right - Field is indeed cunning. [682] \"After I took office, on Crowning's strong recommendation, we conducted a detailed investigation into Field's background. Of course, it was done secretly. Our findings were that Monte Field was born into a New England aristocratic family, one that doesn't boast of being descendants of the Mayflower. He had private tutors as a child, then attended a prestigious private preparatory school, barely graduating. His father, clinging to a glimmer of hope, sent him to Harvard. Field seemed to be a bad egg from a young age. Although he didn't do anything illegal, his behavior was erratic. On the other hand, he had a strong sense of self-worth, so he shortened his last name to Field after a big argument with his father. [683] Quincannon and Ellery nodded. Ellery's eyes reflected with introspection, while Quincannon stared intently at Henry. [684] \"Field,\" Henry continued, \"is not entirely useless. On the contrary, he is very clever. He studied law at Harvard and excelled. He seems to have a talent for rhetoric, of course, thanks to his extensive legal knowledge. But just after graduation, before his family could enjoy any of the fruits of his education, he got involved in a dirty affair with a girl. His father immediately disinherited him. Then he was kicked out - he brought shame to the family - you know...\n[685] \"However, our friend was not defeated by misfortune. He used the little money he had left to venture out and make his own way. However, we don't know how he managed to survive during that time. Next, we learned that he had formed a partnership with a man named Cohen. Cohen was one of the most crooked lawyers around. What a partnership it was! They picked the biggest con artists in the gang and defended them. Through this, they made a lot of money. Now, you should understand how difficult it is to catch someone like that. They knew the loopholes in the law better than even the justices of the Supreme Court. They were not punished - it was a golden age for crime. The con artists felt they were a cut above the rest, as long as Cohen and Field defended them.\" [686] \"Among them, Cohen was experienced and knowledgeable, handling the 'contact' with the company's clients and determining the fees \u2013 despite his imperfect English, he did an excellent job \u2013 oh, and Cohen's fate was also tragic. He was murdered one winter evening, shot in the head. Although it has been twelve years, the killer has not been found. That is \u2013 from a legal standpoint, he has not been found. We have long suspected his identity. With Field's death tonight, I am not surprised that Cohen's case can be dropped. [687] \"So, he was just a playboy,\" Ellery murmured. \"Even after death, his face looks repulsive. I'm sorry I didn't buy the first edition of that novel.\" [688] \"Forget it, bookworm,\" Quin growled. \"Henry, continue.\" [689] \"Now \u2013\" Sampson picked up the last piece of cake from the table and savored it, \"now, let's talk about the good side of Field's life. After his partner's unfortunate death, he seemed to want to change and start anew. He started working \u2013 real legal work \u2013 and he was smart, always able to get things done. For several years, he worked alone, slowly erasing the stain on his reputation in the legal field, even occasionally gaining the respect of some of the proud bigwigs in the field. [690] \"This good performance \u2013 at least on the surface \u2013 lasted for six years. Then, he met Benjamin Morgan \u2013 a reliable, unblemished man with a good reputation, but lacking the talent to become a great lawyer. For some reason, Field convinced Morgan to work with him. After that, things got interesting.\" [691] \"You should remember that there were many illegal things happening in New York at that time, right? We vaguely felt that there was a huge criminal gang consisting of fence, con artists, and lawyers, with some politicians involved in some cases. There were several major robbery cases; bootlegging was rampant in the suburbs. Many people openly robbed and even killed, keeping the police busy. But we all knew that although you caught some of them, you didn't destroy the gang and didn't catch their leader. I have every reason to believe that our late friend, Monte Field, was the mastermind behind the whole thing. [692] \"With such a clever man, doing such things was a piece of cake. With Cohen's guidance, Monte Field had become very familiar with the brains of the entire underworld. After Cohen lost his utility value, he was killed offhand. Then, Field - remember, I'm mainly guessing here, because there's almost no evidence - then, Field openly began to engage in respectable legal affairs while quietly establishing a widespread criminal organization. Of course, we don't know how he did that. When everything was ready and prepared to do something big, he first teamed up with the respected and renowned Morgan, ensuring his legal status, and then began to plan the numerous major fraud cases of the past five years ...\n[693] \"What role did Morgan play in this?\" Ellery asked casually. [694] \"I was just about to talk about Morgan. We have every reason to believe that Morgan is absolutely innocent and has no connection with Field's secret criminal activities. He is a straight man, in fact, he often refuses to defend those with a bad reputation. When Morgan realized what was going on, their relationship must have been very tense. I don't know if that's the case - you can easily get an answer from Morgan. Anyway, they eventually went their separate ways. Since their partnership was dissolved, Field became more and more open, but we still had no concrete evidence and could not bring him to trial. [695] \"Sorry, I interrupt, Henry,\" Quin said thoughtfully, \"Can you provide more information about their breakup? Next time when questioning Morgan, I can also use it to verify what he says.\" [696] \"Of course, I can!\" Henry replied firmly. \"I'm glad you reminded me. Before they finally decided to part ways, they had a big argument that almost came to blows. They were having lunch at the Webster Club when they started arguing fiercely. The argument escalated, and eventually, someone had to intervene. Morgan was furious and even threatened to kill Field. I think Field must have been very calm at that time.\" [697] \"Did anyone witness why they argued?\" Quinny asked. [698] \"Unfortunately, no one knows. The situation quickly calmed down, and they parted ways peacefully. Since then, no one has heard anything about them.\" [699] After the district attorney finished speaking, the room fell into a contemplative silence. Ellery played a few notes of Schubert's music, and Quinny took a small pinch of snuff. [700] \"I would say, although I'm not entirely sure,\" Ellery said, looking up at the ceiling, \"that Morgan is now in big trouble.\" [701] His father muttered. Sampson said solemnly, \"Well, that's your business, gentlemen. I know what to do. Since Field is no longer around, I'll go through his files and records in detail. The rest doesn't matter. I just hope that after Field's murder, his whole gang will come crashing down. I'll send someone to his office tomorrow morning.\" [702] \"One of my men is already there,\" Quin said casually. \"So, you think it's Morgan, right?\" He asked, glancing at Ellery. [703] \"I seem to have already said it,\" Ellery said calmly. \"Basically, Morgan is in trouble now. There's nothing else. I admit, logically speaking, Morgan seems like the killer \u2013 except for one thing, gentlemen.\" [704] \"The hat,\" Quin said quickly. [705] \"No,\" Ellery said. \"Another hat.\" [706] Chapter 7: The Quinns Analyze the Case\n[707] \"Let's see where we stand now,\" Ellery continued. \"From the most basic angle, the facts are as follows: Monte Field was a notorious figure before his death, likely the head of a large criminal organization, and undoubtedly had many enemies. He was murdered at 