ease be rational, Mr. Peacekeeper,\" Ellery said with a smile. \"She didn't tell you about locking the door because you didn't ask her. And she probably felt bad enough already.\" [715] \"The floor lock was a critical piece of evidence that helped us to narrow down the list of suspects to the two ushers, Maggie O'Connell and Jesse Lynch. However, the fact that the\n[716] \"Anyway, through her story, we can rule out the possibility that the killer escaped through the doors near the victim. I admit there are many possibilities - for example, Maggie O'Connell could be an accomplice. I'm just suggesting this as a possibility, not an opinion. At least in my opinion, the killer wouldn't take the risk of being seen leaving through those side doors. Moreover, leaving in an unusual way at an unusual time would be highly noticeable, especially during the second act when almost no one left. Besides, the killer couldn't have known that O'Connell would be neglecting her duties - unless she was an accomplice. Since this case was carefully planned - we must admit, the evidence suggests so - the killer might have given up the idea of escaping through the side doors.\" [717] \"The escape route, I think, is only one left, and that is the main entrance. However, the ticket taker and the doorkeeper outside confirmed that no one left the theater during the second act. Of course, except for the boy selling oranges, who was irrelevant.\" [718] \"All the exits were either guarded or locked, and from 9:35, there was someone in the alley - first Linnie, then Eleanor, then the male usher - Johnny Thurston - and then the police - these facts, along with my questioning and investigation, gentlemen. The inevitable conclusion is that the killer has been in the theater since the body was found!\" [719] After Ellery finished speaking, the room fell silent. \"By the way,\" Ellery added calmly, \"I remember asking the ushers if they saw anyone leave their seats during the second act, and they all said they couldn't remember anyone changing seats.\" [720] Quin absent-mindedly took a pinch of snuff. \"Well done - a reasonable deduction, my son. But ultimately, it's nothing surprising or decisive. Even if the killer was still in the theater, how can we catch him?\" [721] \"He didn't say you could catch him,\" said Sampson, smiling. \"Don't be so sensitive, old friend; nobody will say you were derelict in your duty during the investigation. As far as I know, you handled the whole thing very well tonight.\" [722] Quinn muttered, \"I admit, my not having thoroughly questioned the matter of the door bothers me a bit. But even if the murderer might have left immediately after the crime, I would still have asked the same question, hoping he would still be in the theater.\" [723] \"But, father\u2014of course!\" Ellery said solemnly, \"You have so many things to attend to, and my task is simply to sit back and observe, like Socrates.\" [724] \"So far, how have the people who have been surveyed been doing?\" Sampson curiously asked. [725] \"Hmm, how are they?\" Eleanor questioned, \"We can't seem to get a clear answer from either talking to them or observing their actions. We caught Parsons Johnny, the villain, but it's clear he was just a fan of the show, looking for some juicy gossip about his colleagues. As for Maggie O'Connell, she's suspicious, but we can't make any conclusions at this stage. She could be an accomplice, innocent, or just careless \u2013 she could play any role. Then there's William Pusack, who discovered Field. But have you noticed how slow-witted he is? And Benjamin Morgan \u2013 he's where we found the potential lead. But what can we make of his behavior tonight? Yes, the letter and the free ticket do sound odd, as anyone could have written that letter, even Morgan himself. I must also remember his public threat to kill Field; and the animosity between them, although the reason is unclear, has been going on for two years. Finally, there's Miss Frances Eves-Pope. Unfortunately, I wasn't present when you spoke with her. However, the fact remains that we found her handbag in the deceased's pocket \u2013 isn't that interesting? Could you explain that, please?\" [726] \"So far, that's the situation,\" Ellery continued regretfully. \"We have too many doubts from tonight's case, but too few facts.\" [727] \"So far, son,\" said Quin calmly. \"Your reasoning is flawless. But you've forgotten one important thing\u2014the empty seats. And another surprising fact\u2014the ticket stub of Field, and the only one that could possibly belong to the killer\u2014I mean the LL30 left one that Flint found\u2014these two tickets don't match. That is, the tear marks on the tickets show that the ticket taker checked these two tickets at different times!\" [728] \"Right,\" said Ellery. \"But let's put that aside for now and talk about Field's hat.\" [729] \"That hat\u2014uh, what do you think?\" Quin asked curiously. [730] \"Well, first, we're quite sure the hat didn't disappear by accident. Jesse Lynch saw the dead man's hat on his knees about ten minutes after the second act started. But now the hat is gone, and the only logical explanation is that the killer took it. Now\u2014no matter where the hat is\u2014we can immediately draw one of two conclusions: either the hat itself is connected to the case, and leaving it behind might reveal the killer's identity. We don't know what the connection is yet; or, the hat may contain something the killer wants. You might ask, why didn't the killer take what was inside and leave the hat behind? If this assumption is correct, it may be because he didn't have enough time to take it out, or he didn't know how to take it out, so he took the hat to study it later. Agree with my reasoning so far?\" [731] The district attorney nodded slowly. Quincannon sat motionless, a look of confusion in his eyes. [732] \"Now let's think about what might be in the hat. Ellery continued, wiping his glasses vigorous