afer. And it shouldn't be difficult for the killer to find such a hat, because if he knew its importance beforehand, he would have further investigated the dimensions, style, and other details of Flint's hat. But we don't find a replacement hat here. We have every reason to believe that such a well-planned case should have had a replacement hat. But it's not there, so our only conclusion is that the killer didn't know the importance of the hat beforehand; otherwise, he would have taken preventive measures and left a hat. That way, the police would never know the importance of Flint's hat. [735] \"One more thing to confirm is that even if the killer had some unknown reason not to leave a replacement hat, he would have cut the hat open and\n[736] The district attorney stared at Ellery, his lips pressed together. Detective Quinn appeared to be dozing, his hand hovering between his snuff box and his nose. [737] \"What do you mean, Ellery?\" Sampson asked. \"Why is it so important that the killer didn't know the significance of the hat?\" [738] Ellery smiled. \"The murder occurred during the second act, and I wanted to use that to establish that if the killer didn't know the significance of the hat beforehand, he couldn't have planned the murder during the first intermission... Of course, Fielding's hat could appear somewhere in the theater, and once found, all these theories would be invalid. But... I don't think it will... \"\n[739] \"You've done some basic analysis, but it sounds logical,\" Sampson commended. \"You should become a lawyer.\" [740] \"You're no match for the Quinn family in terms of intellect,\" the old man suddenly broke into a grin. \"But I have to attend to something else related to the hat mystery. Ellery, have you noticed that the tailor's name is sewn onto Fielding's coat?\" [741] \"Let's do it,\" Ellery grinned, pulling out a small notebook from his coat pocket and flipping it open to a blank page. \"Brown Brothers, gentlemen\u2014that's it.\" [74