e one to take it if it's here.\" [760] Sampson nodded. As the three of them left the office and entered the nearly empty theater, Ellery frowned with concern. Occasionally, a busy figure could be seen bending over the seats, carefully inspecting the ground. People kept coming and going from the front boxes. Chief Wiley stood by the main entrance, talking in a low voice with Pigott and Haggstrom. Detective Flint was directing some people to work in the front row of the orchestra. A group of tired janitors used vacuum cleaners to clean the theater. In a corner near the back, a plump female officer was talking to an elderly woman\u2014the same woman Pincus had referred to as Mrs. Philip. [761] Quinn and his companions approached the main entrance. Ellery and Sampson silently watched the empty theater, feeling the oppressive atmosphere. Quinn quickly spoke to Chief Inspector Wiley, whispering orders.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."